var e=[{topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Solubility is the amount of a solute that dissolves in a given amount of solvent at a stated temperature to give a saturated solution. It is a fixed physical property of a drug at a given temperature and pH, not something the formulator invents. Distribution phenomena describe how a drug shares itself between two immiscible liquids, such as oil and water.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The three saturation states`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Unsaturated: contains less dissolved solute than it is capable of holding; more solute will still dissolve.`,`Saturated: the dissolved solute is in equilibrium with undissolved solid; no more will dissolve at that temperature.`,`Supersaturated: contains more dissolved solute than the saturated value, held in a metastable state; a seed crystal or scratch makes it crash out.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ways of expressing solubility`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Molarity (mol/L), molality (mol/kg of solvent), normality and mole fraction are the quantitative expressions.`,`Percentage strength: per cent w/v, w/w or v/v.`,`Parts of solvent required for 1 part of solute - this is the form the pharmacopoeias use, for example "1 g dissolves in 25 mL of water".`,`The Indian Pharmacopoeia and USP also allow descriptive terms, which every GPAT paper tests.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Descriptive term`,`Parts of solvent for 1 part solute`,`Approx. solubility`],rows:[[`Very soluble`,`Less than 1 part`,`More than 1000 mg/mL`],[`Freely soluble`,`1 to 10 parts`,`100 to 1000 mg/mL`],[`Soluble`,`10 to 30 parts`,`33 to 100 mg/mL`],[`Sparingly soluble`,`30 to 100 parts`,`10 to 33 mg/mL`],[`Slightly soluble`,`100 to 1000 parts`,`1 to 10 mg/mL`],[`Very slightly soluble`,`1000 to 10,000 parts`,`0.1 to 1 mg/mL`],[`Practically insoluble / insoluble`,`10,000 parts and over`,`Less than 0.1 mg/mL`]],caption:`IP and USP descriptive solubility terms. The ranges are parts of solvent by volume for one part of solute by weight.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Remember the ladder by its numbers`,text:`The boundaries run 1, 10, 30, 100, 1000, 10,000. Only one boundary is not a power of ten - the 30 that separates soluble from sparingly soluble. Fix that one and the rest fall into place.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What changes a drug solubility`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Nature of solute and solvent: like dissolves like. Polar solvents dissolve polar and ionic solutes; non-polar solvents dissolve non-polar solutes.`,`Temperature: solubility rises with temperature for endothermic dissolution (most solids) and falls for exothermic dissolution (calcium hydroxide, calcium citrate, gases).`,`pH: weak acids dissolve more as pH rises, weak bases dissolve more as pH falls.`,`Particle size: very fine particles below about 1 to 2 micrometres show a genuinely higher saturation solubility (Ostwald-Freundlich effect).`,`Polymorphism: the metastable polymorph is always more soluble and faster dissolving than the stable one; amorphous form is the most soluble of all.`,`Common ion effect and added electrolytes: salting out or salting in.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`dC/dt = (D x A) / (h x V) x (Cs - C)`,caption:`Noyes-Whitney equation (Nernst-Brunner form). dC/dt = dissolution rate; D = diffusion coefficient of drug; A = effective surface area of the solid; h = thickness of the stagnant diffusion layer; V = volume of dissolution medium; Cs = saturation solubility; C = concentration in bulk at time t.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Solubility is not dissolution rate`,text:`Solubility is an equilibrium value (how much). Dissolution rate is a kinetic value (how fast). Micronising a drug does not appreciably change its equilibrium solubility but it multiplies surface area A and therefore the dissolution rate.`}]},{topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you must be able to say which solvent will dissolve which drug from its dielectric constant, predict solubility from pH and pKa, and name the route by which a formulator rescues a poorly soluble molecule. Roughly 40 per cent of new chemical entities are BCS class II, so this is not academic.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Polarity and dielectric constant`},{kind:`para`,text:`The dielectric constant (relative permittivity) measures how well a solvent screens the attraction between opposite charges. A high dielectric constant pulls an ionic crystal apart, so it is a numerical stand-in for polarity. Every drug has a dielectric requirement, that is a value of the dielectric constant of the medium at which its solubility is maximum.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Solvent`,`Dielectric constant (approx., 25 C)`,`Class`],rows:[[`Water`,`80`,`Polar`],[`Glycerin`,`46`,`Polar`],[`Propylene glycol`,`32`,`Semipolar`],[`Ethanol`,`25`,`Semipolar`],[`Acetone`,`21`,`Semipolar`],[`Chloroform`,`5`,`Non-polar`],[`Benzene`,`2.3`,`Non-polar`],[`Liquid paraffin`,`About 2`,`Non-polar`]],caption:`Semipolar solvents such as ethanol act as intermediate solvents, making water and a non-polar liquid mutually more miscible.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`pH and the solubility of ionisable drugs`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Weak acid:  S = S0 (1 + Ka / [H+])      Weak base:  S = S0 (1 + [H+] / Ka)`,caption:`S = total solubility; S0 = intrinsic solubility of the unionised species; Ka = ionisation constant; [H+] = hydrogen ion concentration of the medium.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`pHp (salt of weak acid) = pKa + log [ (S - S0) / S0 ]`,caption:`pHp = the pH below which the free acid precipitates from a solution of its salt. S = concentration of salt in solution; S0 = intrinsic solubility of the free acid. For a salt of a weak base, pHp = pKw - pKb + log [ S0 / (S - S0) ] and precipitation occurs above this pH.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Routes to solubilise a poorly soluble drug`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Approach`,`How it works`,`Pharmaceutical example`],rows:[[`Cosolvency`,`Lowers the dielectric constant of water towards the drug dielectric requirement`,`Ethanol, propylene glycol, glycerin, PEG 400 in elixirs`],[`pH adjustment / salt formation`,`Converts the drug to its ionised, water-soluble form`,`Sodium phenobarbitone, diclofenac sodium`],[`Micellar solubilisation`,`Drug partitions into the hydrophobic core of surfactant micelles above the CMC`,`Polysorbate 80, cetomacrogol in vitamin solutions`],[`Complexation`,`Forms a soluble complex with a ligand or a host cavity`,`Caffeine with benzoic acid; beta-cyclodextrin inclusion complexes`],[`Particle size reduction`,`Raises effective surface area and, below 1 micrometre, saturation solubility`,`Micronised griseofulvin, nanosuspensions`],[`Solid dispersion`,`Presents the drug in the amorphous or molecularly dispersed state`,`Griseofulvin in PEG 6000`],[`Hydrotropy`,`Large amounts of a second solute increase aqueous solubility`,`Sodium benzoate, sodium salicylate, urea`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log S(mix) = f1 log S1 + f2 log S2 + ... + fn log Sn`,caption:`Log-linear cosolvency model. S(mix) = solubility in the blend; f = volume fraction of each solvent; S = solubility of the drug in that pure solvent. Solubility rises roughly exponentially with cosolvent fraction.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Partition coefficient`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`P = C(oil) / C(water)      and      log P = log [ C(oil) / C(water) ]`,caption:`P = partition (distribution) coefficient at equilibrium between two immiscible phases, conventionally n-octanol and water. Higher log P means more lipophilic. Solutes distribute in a fixed ratio at constant temperature - the Nernst distribution law.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The Noyes-Whitney terms you must be able to attack`,text:`A formulator cannot change D or h much. The two workable handles are A (increase by micronisation, wetting agents, effervescence) and Cs (increase by salt formation, metastable polymorph, amorphous form, cosolvent, complexation). Sink conditions mean C is kept below 15 to 20 per cent of Cs so the rate stays maximal.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Which polymorph goes into the tablet`,text:`The metastable polymorph dissolves faster but converts on storage; the stable polymorph is the least soluble but the safest. Amorphous chloramphenicol palmitate (form B) is absorbed, the stable crystalline form A is essentially inactive orally - a classic asked pair.`}]},{topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is quantitative: ideal solubility from thermal data, regular solution theory, and the way association in one phase corrupts a measured partition coefficient.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log Xi = - (dHf / 2.303 R) x [ (T0 - T) / (T0 x T) ]`,caption:`Ideal solubility (van’t Hoff) equation. Xi = ideal solubility as mole fraction; dHf = molar heat of fusion (J/mol); R = 8.314 J/mol/K; T0 = melting point of the solute in kelvin; T = experimental temperature in kelvin. Ideal solubility depends only on the solid, never on the solvent.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A lower melting point and a lower heat of fusion both predict higher solubility - weak crystal lattice, easy to break.`,`At T = T0 the solute is molten and Xi = 1, so a liquid drug is miscible with an ideal solvent in all proportions.`,`Real solubility deviates from ideal through the activity coefficient: log X2 = log Xi - log gamma2.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`- log gamma2 = (V2 x phi1^2 / 2.303 RT) x (d1 - d2)^2`,caption:`Regular (Hildebrand) solution theory. gamma2 = activity coefficient of solute; V2 = molar volume of solute as a liquid; phi1 = volume fraction of solvent; d1 and d2 = solubility parameters of solvent and solute. Solubility is maximum when d1 = d2, that is when the two solubility parameters match.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Partitioning when the solute associates`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`K(true) = C(oil, monomer) / C(water)      K(apparent) = C(oil, total) / C(water)`,caption:`Benzoic acid dimerises in benzene, so the apparent partition coefficient measured from total oil-phase concentration overestimates the true value and is not constant with concentration. Correcting for the degree of dimerisation restores a constant K.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked example - preservative partitioning. A cream contains 40 per cent oil and 60 per cent water. The preservative has an oil/water partition coefficient of 4 and the total concentration is 0.5 per cent w/v.`,`Concentration in the aqueous phase Cw = C(total) / (Ko/w x fraction oil + fraction water) = 0.5 / (4 x 0.4 + 0.6).`,`Denominator = 1.6 + 0.6 = 2.2, so Cw = 0.5 / 2.2 = 0.227 per cent w/v.`,`Only the aqueous free fraction preserves, so a high partition coefficient preservative is largely wasted in the oil phase - this is why chloroform and phenolic preservatives fail in high-oil creams.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Noyes-Whitney term`,`Meaning`,`Formulation lever`],rows:[[`D`,`Diffusion coefficient of drug in the medium`,`Falls if medium viscosity rises (sucrose syrups slow dissolution)`],[`A`,`Effective wetted surface area`,`Micronisation, surfactant wetting, disintegrant, effervescence`],[`h`,`Stagnant diffusion layer thickness`,`Falls with agitation and higher paddle speed`],[`Cs`,`Saturation solubility`,`Salt form, polymorph, amorphous, cosolvent, complexation, pH`],[`C`,`Bulk concentration at time t`,`Kept low by sink conditions and by absorption in vivo`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Solubility of gases and the small print`,text:`Gas solubility follows Henry law: solubility is proportional to partial pressure. Gas solubility falls with rising temperature and with added electrolyte (salting out), which is why water for injection is boiled and cooled under carbon dioxide free conditions before dissolving oxygen-sensitive drugs.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Salting in and salting out`,text:`Salting out is the fall in solubility of a non-electrolyte caused by an added electrolyte that competes for water of hydration (sodium chloride precipitating a protein). Salting in is the opposite, seen with large anions such as benzoate, salicylate and iodide, which open water structure and raise solubility.`}]},{topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A molecule inside a liquid is pulled equally in every direction by its neighbours. A molecule at the surface has no neighbours above it, so it is pulled inwards. That unbalanced inward pull is what makes a liquid surface behave like a stretched skin, and it is the origin of surface tension.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Surface tension versus interfacial tension`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Surface tension (gamma) is the force per unit length acting at a liquid-gas (liquid-air) surface. Units: dyne/cm, or mN/m in SI. The two are numerically equal.`,`Interfacial tension is the same force at the boundary between two immiscible liquids, or a liquid and a solid.`,`Interfacial tension is almost always lower than the surface tension of the more polar liquid, because the two phases still attract each other across the boundary.`,`Surface free energy is the work needed to make new surface: W = gamma x change in area, expressed in erg/cm2 or mJ/m2.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`System`,`Tension at 20 C (dyne/cm)`,`Type`],rows:[[`Water - air`,`72.8`,`Surface tension`],[`Benzene - air`,`28.9`,`Surface tension`],[`Mercury - air`,`About 476`,`Surface tension`],[`Water - benzene`,`35.0`,`Interfacial tension`],[`Water - chloroform`,`32.8`,`Interfacial tension`],[`Water - olive oil`,`22.9`,`Interfacial tension`],[`Water - n-octanol`,`8.5`,`Interfacial tension`]],caption:`Water has an unusually high surface tension because of hydrogen bonding; mercury has the highest of common liquids because of metallic bonding.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Surfactants: the molecules that lower it`},{kind:`para`,text:`A surface active agent (surfactant) has a water-loving head and an oil-loving tail in the same molecule. It cannot be comfortable in either phase alone, so it collects at the interface. Once there, it replaces high-energy water-air or water-oil contacts with low-energy contacts and the tension falls.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Surfactants lower surface and interfacial tension and are positively adsorbed at the interface.`,`Inorganic electrolytes such as sodium chloride raise surface tension slightly and are negatively adsorbed (pushed into the bulk).`,`Above a certain concentration the surfactant molecules cluster into micelles - this concentration is the critical micelle concentration (CMC).`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`What happens exactly at the CMC`,text:`Surface tension stops falling and plateaus, turbidity and light scattering rise sharply, solubilisation of water-insoluble material begins, osmotic pressure levels off, and equivalent conductivity of an ionic surfactant drops abruptly. A break in any of these plots against concentration marks the CMC.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Adsorption versus absorption`,text:`Adsorption is a surface phenomenon (ad = at the surface, molecules stick on). Absorption is bulk uptake (throughout). Sorption is when both happen together.`}]},{topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This is the most formula-dense topic in physical pharmaceutics. You need the four measurement methods and which is suitable where, the three adsorption isotherms in linear form, the surfactant classes with real examples, and enough HLB arithmetic to blend two emulsifiers.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Methods of measuring surface and interfacial tension`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`Working equation`,`Suitable for`],rows:[[`Capillary rise`,`gamma = (1/2) x r x h x rho x g`,`Surface tension only, not interfacial`],[`Drop weight / drop count (stalagmometer)`,`gamma = m x g / (2 x pi x r x F)`,`Surface and interfacial tension`],[`du Nouy ring tensiometer`,`gamma = (F x beta) / (4 x pi x R)`,`Surface and interfacial tension`],[`Wilhelmy plate`,`gamma = F / (2 x (l + t))`,`Surface and interfacial, dynamic studies`]],caption:`r = capillary radius; h = rise height; rho = density; g = 9.81 m/s2; m = drop mass; F in the ring equation = detachment force; beta = Harkins-Jordan correction factor; R = ring radius; l and t = plate length and thickness. The 4 x pi x R in the ring method appears because the liquid film has two surfaces on a ring of circumference 2 x pi x R.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Capillary rise cannot do interfaces`,text:`Capillary rise is the most accurate method but it is restricted to liquid-air surface tension and needs a zero contact angle. If the question says interfacial tension between two liquids, capillary rise is the wrong answer - choose the du Nouy ring, drop weight or Wilhelmy plate.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Adsorption isotherms`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Isotherm`,`Equation`,`Linear form`,`Key assumption`],rows:[[`Freundlich`,`x/m = k x C^(1/n)`,`log (x/m) = log k + (1/n) log C`,`Empirical, heterogeneous surface, no saturation`],[`Langmuir`,`y = (a x b x C) / (1 + b x C)`,`C/y = 1/(a x b) + C/a`,`Monolayer on identical sites, no lateral interaction`],[`BET`,`Multilayer extension of Langmuir`,`p / [ V(p0 - p) ] = 1/(Vm x c) + (c - 1)p / (Vm x c x p0)`,`Multilayer adsorption; used for surface area`]],caption:`x/m = amount adsorbed per gram of adsorbent; C = equilibrium concentration; k and n = Freundlich constants; a = amount for a complete monolayer; b = affinity constant; Vm = monolayer volume; p/p0 = relative pressure.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A Freundlich plot of log (x/m) against log C gives slope 1/n and intercept log k.`,`A Langmuir plot of C/y against C gives slope 1/a, so the monolayer capacity is the reciprocal of the slope.`,`BET with nitrogen at its boiling point is the standard method for specific surface area of powders.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Surfactant classification`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Charge on the surface-active part`,`Examples`,`Chief use`],rows:[[`Anionic`,`Negative`,`Sodium lauryl sulphate, sodium docusate, soaps, sodium stearate`,`Detergent, wetting, O/W emulsifier`],[`Cationic`,`Positive`,`Benzalkonium chloride, cetrimide, cetylpyridinium chloride`,`Antiseptic, preservative, O/W emulsifier`],[`Non-ionic`,`None`,`Spans (sorbitan esters), Tweens (polysorbates), cetomacrogol, poloxamers, glyceryl monostearate`,`Least toxic, least irritant, widest use`],[`Amphoteric / zwitterionic`,`Depends on pH`,`Lecithin, cocamidopropyl betaine, N-dodecyl alanine`,`Mild shampoos, isoelectric behaviour`]],caption:`Non-ionic surfactants are unaffected by pH and electrolytes, which is why they dominate parenteral and ophthalmic use.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The HLB system`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`HLB = 20 x (Mh / M)      and for polyhydric alcohol fatty acid esters:  HLB = 20 x (1 - S/A)`,caption:`Griffin scale. Mh = molecular weight of the hydrophilic portion; M = total molecular weight; S = saponification value of the ester; A = acid value of the recovered fatty acid. Davies method: HLB = 7 + sum of hydrophilic group numbers - sum of lipophilic group numbers.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`HLB range`,`Application`],rows:[[`1 to 3`,`Antifoaming agent`],[`3 to 6`,`Water-in-oil (W/O) emulsifying agent`],[`7 to 9`,`Wetting and spreading agent`],[`8 to 18`,`Oil-in-water (O/W) emulsifying agent`],[`13 to 15`,`Detergent`],[`15 to 18`,`Solubilising agent`]],caption:`Span 80 has HLB 4.3 (W/O), Tween 80 has HLB 15.0 (O/W), Span 20 has HLB 8.6, Tween 20 has HLB 16.7.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`HLB (blend) = fA x HLB(A) + fB x HLB(B),  where fA + fB = 1`,caption:`Blending rule. f = weight fraction of each surfactant in the mixed emulsifier. Solve for fA when the required HLB of the oil phase is known.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`A blend beats a single surfactant`,text:`A mixture of a high-HLB and a low-HLB emulsifier giving the required HLB produces a more stable, more closely packed interfacial film than a single surfactant of the same HLB. Tween 80 with Span 80 is the classic pair.`}]},{topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is the thermodynamics: Gibbs adsorption, the spreading coefficient and its relationship to work of adhesion and cohesion, and the quantitative behaviour of micellar systems.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Gamma = - (1 / RT) x (d gamma / d ln C) = - (C / RT) x (d gamma / dC)`,caption:`Gibbs adsorption equation. Gamma (capital) = surface excess concentration, mol/cm2; gamma (small) = surface tension; C = bulk concentration; R = gas constant; T = absolute temperature. A negative d gamma/dC (tension falls as concentration rises) gives a positive surface excess, that is positive adsorption.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Surfactants: d gamma/dC is negative, so Gamma is positive - they concentrate at the surface.`,`Inorganic electrolytes and sucrose: d gamma/dC is positive, so Gamma is negative - they are driven into the bulk.`,`Above the CMC surface tension is almost constant, so d gamma/dC approaches zero and the surface is saturated with a close-packed monolayer. Area per molecule = 1 / (Gamma x Avogadro number).`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Spreading coefficient`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`S = gamma(S) - [ gamma(L) + gamma(LS) ]      equivalently  S = Wa - Wc`,caption:`S = spreading coefficient of liquid L on sublayer S; gamma(S) = surface tension of the sublayer; gamma(L) = surface tension of the spreading liquid; gamma(LS) = interfacial tension between them. Wa = work of adhesion = gamma(L) + gamma(S) - gamma(LS); Wc = work of cohesion of the spreading liquid = 2 x gamma(L).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`S positive: the liquid spreads spontaneously into a film (adhesion beats cohesion).`,`S negative: the liquid stays as a lens or discrete drops.`,`Oleic acid on water: S = 72.8 - (32.5 + 15.6) = +24.7 dyne/cm, so it spreads. Liquid petrolatum on water: S = 72.8 - (33.1 + 57.0) = -17.3 dyne/cm, so it does not.`,`A liquid that does not spread initially may spread after mutual saturation changes the tensions - benzene on water is the classic case.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Micellar quantities worth knowing`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Quantity`,`Meaning`,`Typical fact`],rows:[[`CMC`,`Concentration at which micelles first form`,`Sodium lauryl sulphate about 8.1 mmol/L at 25 C`],[`Aggregation number`,`Number of monomers per micelle`,`About 62 for sodium lauryl sulphate`],[`Krafft point`,`Temperature above which an ionic surfactant micellises`,`Below it, solubility is too low to reach the CMC`],[`Cloud point`,`Temperature at which a non-ionic surfactant solution turns turbid`,`Polysorbates and polyoxyethylene ethers; dehydration of the PEG chain`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`CMC falls as the hydrocarbon chain lengthens, and falls sharply on adding electrolyte for ionic surfactants (the counter-ions screen head-group repulsion).`,`Non-ionic surfactants have much lower CMC values than ionic surfactants of the same chain length.`,`Above the CMC, added surfactant goes into new micelles, so the monomer concentration stays essentially constant.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Wetting is a contact angle question`,text:`Young equation: gamma(S) = gamma(SL) + gamma(L) cos theta. Complete wetting is theta = 0 degrees; no wetting is theta = 180 degrees; a powder with theta above 90 degrees floats. Wetting agents (HLB 7 to 9) work by lowering gamma(L) and gamma(SL), thereby lowering theta - they do not change the solid.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Adsorption at solid surfaces`,text:`Physisorption is weak (van der Waals), reversible, multilayer, low heat of adsorption (under 40 kJ/mol) and increases as temperature falls. Chemisorption is strong, specific, monolayer, irreversible, with a high heat of adsorption. Activated charcoal in poisoning is physisorption.`}]},{topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A dispersed system is one substance broken up and scattered through another. Sort them by particle size: a true solution has particles below 1 nanometre, a colloidal dispersion has particles roughly 1 nanometre to 0.5 micrometre, and a coarse dispersion (suspension or emulsion) has particles above about 0.5 micrometre. Colloids are invisible under an ordinary microscope but they scatter light.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Three kinds of colloid`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Solvent affinity`,`Formation`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Lyophilic (hydrophilic in water)`,`Strong, solvated`,`Spontaneous on adding solvent`,`Acacia, tragacanth, gelatin, methylcellulose, proteins`],[`Lyophobic (hydrophobic)`,`Little or none`,`Needs special dispersion methods`,`Gold, silver, sulphur sols, silver iodide`],[`Association (amphiphilic)`,`Both regions in one molecule`,`Spontaneous above the CMC`,`Surfactant micelles - polysorbates, sodium lauryl sulphate`]],caption:`Lyophilic sols are thermodynamically stable and reversible; lyophobic sols are unstable, irreversible and need a stabiliser.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Lyophilic sols are stabilised mainly by the solvation layer, so they tolerate small amounts of electrolyte; large amounts salt them out.`,`Lyophobic sols are stabilised only by charge, so a trace of electrolyte coagulates them.`,`A lyophilic colloid added to a lyophobic sol to protect it is called a protective colloid; its power is expressed as the gold number (the lower the gold number, the better the protection).`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`How we detect and measure colloids`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Optical: the Tyndall effect - a beam of light passing through a colloidal sol is scattered and becomes visible as a luminous cone. True solutions do not show it. The ultramicroscope and the electron microscope exploit this.`,`Kinetic: Brownian motion, diffusion, sedimentation, osmotic pressure and viscosity. Brownian motion is the random zig-zag movement caused by bombardment by solvent molecules.`,`Electrical: colloidal particles carry a surface charge, so they move in an electric field (electrophoresis).`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Suspensions and emulsions in one line each`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A suspension is a solid dispersed in a liquid; the main problems are caking and slow redispersion.`,`An emulsion is one liquid dispersed as globules in another immiscible liquid, stabilised by an emulsifying agent. Oil-in-water (O/W) has oil as the internal phase; water-in-oil (W/O) has water inside.`,`Both are thermodynamically unstable because of the large interfacial area created, and both need an added agent to keep them dispersed.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`v = d^2 x (rho(s) - rho(l)) x g / (18 x eta)`,caption:`Stokes law. v = sedimentation (settling) velocity, cm/s; d = particle diameter, cm; rho(s) and rho(l) = densities of solid and liquid, g/cm3; g = 981 cm/s2; eta = viscosity of the medium, poise. Settling is slowed by reducing d, reducing the density difference, or raising viscosity.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Stokes law is a squared law`,text:`Halving the particle diameter cuts the settling velocity to one quarter, not one half. This is the single most commonly examined consequence of the equation.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Which phase is which`,text:`In O/W the first letter is the internal (dispersed) phase and the last is the external (continuous) phase. O/W means oil droplets floating in water, so it is water-washable, non-greasy and conducts electricity.`}]},{topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam-level material is the electrical double layer with zeta potential, DLVO theory, the flocculated versus deflocculated comparison, emulsion identification tests, and the two suspension parameters F and beta.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Electrical double layer and zeta potential`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`The particle surface acquires a charge by ion adsorption, ionisation of surface groups, or ion dissolution.`,`Tightly held counter-ions form the Stern layer, giving the Stern (or Nernst-adjacent) potential.`,`Loosely held counter-ions form the diffuse Gouy-Chapman layer that fades to zero in the bulk.`,`The plane of shear lies just outside the Stern layer; the potential there is the zeta (electrokinetic) potential - the only one that can be measured, by electrophoresis.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Electrokinetic phenomenon`,`What moves`,`What is applied`],rows:[[`Electrophoresis`,`Charged particles through a stationary liquid`,`Electric field`],[`Electroosmosis`,`Liquid through a fixed charged bed or membrane`,`Electric field`],[`Sedimentation potential (Dorn effect)`,`Particles settle, potential appears`,`Gravity / centrifugal force`],[`Streaming potential`,`Liquid forced through a plug, potential appears`,`Pressure`]],caption:`The first two apply a field and get motion; the last two apply motion and get a field.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`V(T) = V(R) + V(A)`,caption:`DLVO theory. V(T) = total potential energy of interaction between two particles; V(R) = electrostatic repulsion, falls off exponentially with distance; V(A) = van der Waals attraction, dominant at very short and at long distances. The curve shows a primary maximum (energy barrier), a deep primary minimum (caking, irreversible) and a shallow secondary minimum (loose flocculation, reversible).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Flocculated versus deflocculated suspensions`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Deflocculated`,`Flocculated`],rows:[[`Particle state`,`Separate individual particles`,`Loose aggregates (flocs)`],[`Rate of settling`,`Slow`,`Fast`],[`Supernatant`,`Cloudy for a long time`,`Clear rapidly`],[`Sediment volume F`,`Low`,`High, close to 1`],[`Sediment nature`,`Hard, dense cake`,`Loose, fluffy, scaffold-like`],[`Redispersion`,`Very difficult or impossible`,`Easy on shaking`],[`Zeta potential`,`High (about 25 mV or more)`,`Reduced (about 20 mV or less)`],[`Pharmaceutical elegance`,`Good appearance, bad caking`,`Poor appearance, good redispersibility`]],caption:`Controlled flocculation is the compromise: flocculate the suspension, then add a structured vehicle so the fast-settling flocs stay suspended.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`F = Vu / Vo      and      beta = F / F(infinity)`,caption:`F = sedimentation volume = final sediment volume Vu divided by original suspension volume Vo. F ranges from near 0 to 1 and above (F greater than 1 means the sediment swells above the original volume). beta = degree of flocculation = F of the flocculated suspension divided by F(infinity), the sedimentation volume of the same suspension when completely deflocculated.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Identifying an emulsion type`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test`,`O/W result`,`W/O result`],rows:[[`Dilution test`,`Dilutes with water`,`Dilutes with oil`],[`Dye solubility (Scarlet red / Sudan III, oil soluble)`,`Scattered red globules`,`Continuous red background`],[`Dye solubility (amaranth / methylene blue, water soluble)`,`Continuous coloured background`,`Scattered coloured globules`],[`Conductivity test`,`Conducts, lamp glows`,`Does not conduct`],[`Filter paper / cobalt chloride paper`,`Blue paper turns pink`,`Paper stays blue`],[`Fluorescence test`,`Spotty fluorescence`,`Whole field fluoresces`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ways an emulsion fails`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Instability`,`What happens`,`Reversible on shaking?`],rows:[[`Creaming`,`Globules rise (or sedimentation, they fall) but stay separate`,`Yes`],[`Flocculation`,`Globules clump but the interfacial film is intact`,`Yes`],[`Coalescence / cracking (breaking)`,`Films rupture, globules merge, phases separate`,`No`],[`Phase inversion`,`O/W becomes W/O or the reverse`,`No, needs reformulation`],[`Ostwald ripening`,`Small globules dissolve and redeposit on large ones`,`No`]],caption:`Creaming follows Stokes law and is only a cosmetic and dosing problem; cracking is irreversible and is the true failure.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`What inverts an emulsion`,text:`Adding an electrolyte with a polyvalent cation (calcium chloride to a sodium stearate stabilised O/W emulsion converts it to calcium stearate, a W/O emulsifier), changing the phase volume ratio beyond about 74 per cent, temperature change, or adding an opposite-type emulsifier.`}]},{topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is quantitative: Stokes law arithmetic, sedimentation parameters worked out, Schulze-Hardy behaviour of coagulating electrolytes, and the theories of emulsion stabilisation.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked Stokes example. Particles of diameter 20 micrometres, particle density 2.0 g/cm3, medium density 1.0 g/cm3, medium viscosity 0.01 poise.`,`Convert diameter: 20 micrometres = 20 x 10^-4 cm = 2 x 10^-3 cm, so d squared = 4 x 10^-6 cm2.`,`Numerator = 4 x 10^-6 x (2.0 - 1.0) x 981 = 3.924 x 10^-3.`,`Denominator = 18 x 0.01 = 0.18. Therefore v = 3.924 x 10^-3 / 0.18 = 0.0218 cm/s, roughly 1.3 cm per minute.`,`Raise the viscosity tenfold to 0.1 poise and v falls tenfold to 0.00218 cm/s - this is why suspending agents work.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked sedimentation example. 100 mL of suspension settles to 25 mL of sediment when flocculated, and to 10 mL when fully deflocculated.`,`F (flocculated) = 25 / 100 = 0.25.`,`F(infinity) (deflocculated) = 10 / 100 = 0.10.`,`Degree of flocculation beta = 0.25 / 0.10 = 2.5, meaning the flocculated system has 2.5 times the sediment volume of the deflocculated one.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Coagulation of lyophobic sols`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Schulze-Hardy rule: the coagulating power of an electrolyte rises steeply with the valency of the ion opposite in charge to the particle. For a negative sol the order is Al3+ greater than Ca2+ greater than Na+, roughly in the ratio 1000 : 100 : 1 (a sixth-power relationship in DLVO terms).`,`Critical coagulation concentration is the minimum electrolyte concentration that just coagulates the sol.`,`Hofmeister (lyotropic) series ranks ions for their salting-out power on lyophilic colloids: citrate greater than tartrate greater than sulphate greater than acetate greater than chloride greater than nitrate greater than iodide greater than thiocyanate.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Theories of emulsion stabilisation`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Theory`,`Mechanism`,`Agent`],rows:[[`Monomolecular film`,`Surfactant forms a coherent, flexible film at the interface`,`Tweens, Spans, soaps`],[`Multimolecular film`,`Hydrophilic colloid forms a thick multilayer coat and raises viscosity`,`Acacia, gelatin, tragacanth`],[`Solid particle film`,`Finely divided solid wetted by both phases sits at the interface`,`Bentonite, magnesium hydroxide, veegum`]],caption:`The phase that preferentially wets the solid particle becomes the external phase - bentonite (water-wetted) gives an O/W emulsion.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Zeta potential zero is not the goal`,text:`At zero zeta potential (the isoelectric point) attraction is maximal and the suspension crashes into the deep primary minimum, giving a hard cake. Controlled flocculation aims at a small but non-zero zeta potential, around plus or minus 20 mV, so particles sit in the shallow secondary minimum and redisperse.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Numbers worth memorising`,text:`Colloidal size range 1 nm to 0.5 micrometre; maximum theoretical close packing of uniform spheres 74 per cent by volume (above this an emulsion inverts); a good pharmaceutical suspension has F approaching 1; degree of flocculation beta is always greater than 1 for a flocculated system.`}]},{topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Rheology is the study of flow and deformation of matter. In pharmacy it decides whether a syrup pours, whether a cream spreads, whether a suspension resists settling and whether a paste squeezes out of a tube. Viscosity is the resistance of a fluid to flow - the internal friction between adjacent layers.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The basic quantities`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Quantity`,`Symbol`,`Definition`,`Unit`],rows:[[`Shear stress`,`F/A or tau`,`Force per unit area applied`,`dyne/cm2 or Pa`],[`Shear rate`,`dv/dr or G`,`Velocity gradient between layers`,`per second (s-1)`],[`Viscosity (dynamic)`,`eta`,`Shear stress divided by shear rate`,`poise (dyne s/cm2); 1 Pa.s = 10 poise`],[`Fluidity`,`phi`,`Reciprocal of viscosity, 1/eta`,`per poise`],[`Kinematic viscosity`,`nu`,`Viscosity divided by density, eta/rho`,`stokes (cm2/s)`]],caption:`Water has a viscosity of about 1 centipoise (0.01 poise) at 20 C. Glycerin is about 400 centipoise.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`eta = F / G = shear stress / shear rate`,caption:`Newton law of flow. For a Newtonian liquid eta is a constant at a given temperature, so a plot of shear rate against shear stress is a straight line through the origin.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Newtonian and non-Newtonian`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Newtonian: viscosity does not change with shear rate. Water, glycerin, syrup, alcohol, mineral oil, chloroform, castor oil - simple liquids and true solutions.`,`Non-Newtonian: viscosity changes with the applied shear rate. All colloidal dispersions, emulsions, suspensions, ointments, gels and pastes.`,`Non-Newtonian systems need a single-point measurement to be reported with the shear rate at which it was made, otherwise the number is meaningless.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Pseudoplastic versus dilatant`,text:`Pseudoplastic = shear thinning = gets thinner when you stir (most polymer gels, "shake it and it pours"). Dilatant = shear thickening = gets thicker when you stir (a concentrated starch or zinc oxide slurry that sets solid if stirred fast).`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why we want shear thinning`,text:`A pseudoplastic or plastic vehicle is thick on the shelf (holds particles up), thin during shaking and pouring (dosable), and thick again in the bottle. That combination is the whole reason rheology is on the syllabus.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Viscosity of a liquid falls as temperature rises; viscosity of a gas rises as temperature rises.`,`Viscosity of a liquid varies with temperature according to an Arrhenius-type equation, eta = A x e^(Ev/RT).`]}]},{topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core is the three non-Newtonian flow types with their rheograms and examples, the time-dependent phenomena, and which viscometer can legitimately be used for which system.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The three non-Newtonian flow types`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Flow type`,`Rheogram`,`Viscosity with rising shear`,`Yield value`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Plastic (Bingham body)`,`Straight line meeting stress axis above origin`,`Constant once flow starts`,`Yes`,`Concentrated flocculated suspensions, toothpaste, zinc oxide paste`],[`Pseudoplastic (shear thinning)`,`Curve starting at the origin, concave to stress axis`,`Decreases`,`No`,`Acacia, tragacanth, sodium alginate, methylcellulose, CMC solutions`],[`Dilatant (shear thickening)`,`Curve starting at the origin, convex to stress axis`,`Increases`,`No`,`Suspensions with over 50 per cent deflocculated solids, starch paste, zinc oxide in water`]],caption:`Only plastic flow has a yield value. Pseudoplastic and dilatant curves both pass through the origin.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Plastic:  U = (F - f) / G      Pseudoplastic:  F^N = eta x G  (N greater than 1)      Dilatant:  F^N = eta x G  (N less than 1)`,caption:`U = plastic viscosity or mobility; f = yield value, the stress that must be exceeded before flow begins; F = shear stress; G = shear rate; N = an index of deviation from Newtonian flow (N = 1 is Newtonian).`},{kind:`para`,text:`The yield value f is the pressure needed to break the loose flocculated structure and start flow. Below f the material behaves as an elastic solid. Toothpaste has a yield value, which is why it sits on the brush instead of running off.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Time-dependent behaviour`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Phenomenon`,`What happens`,`Rheogram sign`,`Example`],rows:[[`Thixotropy`,`Isothermal gel-to-sol-to-gel; structure rebuilds slowly on standing`,`Hysteresis loop, down-curve left of up-curve`,`Bentonite magma, procaine penicillin G suspension`],[`Antithixotropy (negative thixotropy)`,`Viscosity increases with time of shearing, then recovers`,`Down-curve right of up-curve`,`Dilute magnesia magma`],[`Rheopexy`,`A sol becomes a gel faster when gently sheared than when left standing`,`Structure builds under mild shear`,`Some bentonite dispersions under gentle rolling`]],caption:`Thixotropy is desirable in suspensions and in intramuscular depot injections; the area of the hysteresis loop measures the extent of thixotropic breakdown.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Viscometers - the decisive table`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Viscometer`,`Principle`,`Newtonian only?`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Capillary (Ostwald / U-tube)`,`Time for a fixed volume to flow through a capillary`,`Newtonian only`,`Shear rate varies across the tube`],[`Falling sphere (Hoeppler)`,`Time for a ball to fall through the liquid, Stokes law`,`Newtonian only`,`Needs a transparent liquid`],[`Cup and bob (Couette, Searle, Stormer, Haake)`,`Torque on a bob rotating in a cup of sample`,`Newtonian and non-Newtonian`,`Plug flow can occur with plastic systems`],[`Cone and plate (Ferranti-Shirley, Brookfield CP)`,`Torque on a shallow cone over a flat plate`,`Newtonian and non-Newtonian`,`Constant shear rate everywhere, tiny sample, best for thixotropy`]],caption:`The single most asked point: capillary and falling-sphere viscometers are limited to Newtonian systems; only multipoint instruments (cup-and-bob, cone-and-plate) can characterise non-Newtonian flow.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`eta = (pi x r^4 x dP x t) / (8 x l x V)`,caption:`Poiseuille equation, the basis of capillary viscometry. r = capillary radius; dP = pressure difference driving flow; t = time of flow; l = capillary length; V = volume flowing. Note the fourth-power dependence on radius.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`eta1 / eta2 = (rho1 x t1) / (rho2 x t2)`,caption:`Working comparison used with an Ostwald viscometer: the unknown liquid (1) and a reference such as water (2) run through the same capillary under their own heads, so viscosity ratio equals the product of density and flow time ratio.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Thixotropy is not pseudoplasticity`,text:`Pseudoplasticity is shear-rate dependence (thins when sheared harder, recovers instantly). Thixotropy is time dependence (thins when sheared for longer, recovers slowly). A system can be pseudoplastic without being thixotropic, and thixotropy is always superimposed on plastic or pseudoplastic flow.`}]},{topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced rheology asks for arithmetic on viscosity relationships, viscoelastic terminology and the specific instrument choices for structured pharmaceutical systems.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Viscosity of dispersions`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`eta = eta(0) x (1 + 2.5 x phi)`,caption:`Einstein equation for a dilute dispersion of rigid spheres. eta = viscosity of the dispersion; eta(0) = viscosity of the pure continuous phase; phi = volume fraction of the dispersed phase (valid up to about 2 per cent). Viscosity depends on volume fraction, not on particle size.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Derived viscosity term`,`Expression`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Relative viscosity`,`eta / eta(0)`,`Dispersion viscosity as a multiple of the vehicle`],[`Specific viscosity`,`(eta / eta(0)) - 1`,`Fractional increase caused by the dispersed phase`],[`Reduced viscosity`,`eta(sp) / c`,`Specific viscosity per unit concentration`],[`Intrinsic viscosity`,`Limit of eta(sp)/c as c approaches 0`,`Used to estimate polymer molecular weight`]],caption:`The Mark-Houwink relationship, intrinsic viscosity = K x M^a, links intrinsic viscosity to molecular weight M.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked Ostwald example. Water: density 1.00 g/cm3, flow time 30 s, viscosity 1.0 centipoise. Test liquid: density 1.20 g/cm3, flow time 75 s.`,`Apply eta1 = eta2 x (rho1 x t1) / (rho2 x t2) = 1.0 x (1.20 x 75) / (1.00 x 30).`,`Numerator = 90; denominator = 30; so eta1 = 1.0 x 3 = 3.0 centipoise.`,`Kinematic viscosity of the test liquid = 3.0 / 1.20 = 2.5 centistokes.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Viscoelasticity`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Creep testing applies a constant stress and follows strain with time; the creep compliance curve resolves into instantaneous elastic, viscoelastic and Newtonian regions.`,`Oscillatory testing gives the storage modulus G prime (elastic, solid-like) and the loss modulus G double prime (viscous, liquid-like). A gel has G prime greater than G double prime.`,`The Maxwell model (spring and dashpot in series) and the Kelvin-Voigt model (in parallel) are the mechanical analogues.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Plug flow`,text:`In a cup-and-bob viscometer a plastic material near the bob may be sheared while material further away stays unyielded and moves as a solid plug. This falsely lowers the apparent yield value. Cone-and-plate avoids it because the shear rate is uniform throughout the gap.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Units that catch people out`,text:`1 poise = 1 dyne s/cm2 = 0.1 Pa.s, so 1 Pa.s = 10 poise and 1 centipoise = 1 mPa.s. Kinematic viscosity in stokes = viscosity in poise divided by density in g/cm3; 1 stoke = 100 centistokes.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ointments and creams are usually plastic with thixotropy; the ideal semisolid has a low yield value and marked thixotropy.`,`Nasal and ophthalmic gels exploit pseudoplasticity so they spread on shear but resist drainage afterwards.`,`Dilatancy is a manufacturing hazard: a dilatant slurry can seize a high-speed mixer or a granulator, so processing must be done at low shear.`]}]},{topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Micromeritics, a term coined by Dalla Valle, is the science of small particles. It covers particle size and its distribution, particle shape, surface area, density, porosity and powder flow. Almost every unit operation in tablet and capsule manufacture depends on these properties.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why particle size matters`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Dissolution rate and hence bioavailability - smaller particles have more surface area (griseofulvin, digoxin, spironolactone).`,`Content uniformity, especially for low-dose potent drugs.`,`Flow into the die and therefore weight variation of tablets.`,`Stability of suspensions and emulsions (Stokes law) and of aerosols (deposition in the lung).`,`Texture, grittiness and feel of ointments and ophthalmic products.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Expressing size`},{kind:`para`,text:`A real powder contains particles of many sizes, so a single number cannot describe it. We report a mean diameter together with a measure of spread. When the particles are not spherical we use an equivalent diameter - the diameter of a sphere that has the same volume, the same surface area, or the same settling velocity as the particle.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Projected (Feret and Martin) diameters come from microscopy.`,`Stokes diameter comes from sedimentation methods.`,`Volume diameter comes from the Coulter counter.`,`Sieve diameter is the width of the minimum square aperture the particle passes through.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The four densities`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Density`,`Volume it uses`,`Measured by`],rows:[[`True (absolute) density`,`Solid material only, no pores at all`,`Helium pycnometer`],[`Granule density`,`Solid plus sealed intraparticle pores`,`Mercury displacement pycnometer`],[`Bulk (poured) density`,`Solid plus all pores plus spaces between particles`,`Pouring into a graduated cylinder`],[`Tapped density`,`Same, after a standard number of taps`,`Tapped density apparatus`]],caption:`The order is always true density greater than granule density greater than tapped density greater than bulk density.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Porosity (e) = (Vb - Vp) / Vb = 1 - (Vp / Vb) = 1 - (bulk density / true density)`,caption:`Vb = bulk volume of the powder bed; Vp = true volume of the particles themselves. Porosity is usually reported as a percentage of void space.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Flow in one sentence`,text:`Good flow means a low angle of repose, a low Carr index and a low Hausner ratio. Everything about powder flow follows from that one relationship.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Tapped over bulk`,text:`Hausner ratio = Tapped divided by Bulk - "T over B". Since tapped is always the larger, the ratio is always above 1; the closer to 1, the better the flow.`}]},{topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core is the mean diameters, which sizing method covers which size range, and the flow property table with its numerical bands.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mean diameters`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`d(mean) = [ sum(n x d^(p + f)) / sum(n x d^f) ] ^ (1/p)`,caption:`Edmundson general equation. n = number of particles in a size range; d = mid-point diameter of that range; p = an index for the property (1 for length, 2 for surface, 3 for volume); f = a frequency index (0 for number distribution, 3 for volume weighting).`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Mean diameter`,`p`,`f`,`What it emphasises`],rows:[[`Length-number mean, d(ln)`,`1`,`0`,`Simple arithmetic mean of diameters`],[`Surface-number mean, d(sn)`,`2`,`0`,`Surface area, counted by number`],[`Volume-number mean, d(vn)`,`3`,`0`,`Volume or mass, counted by number`],[`Volume-surface (Sauter) mean, d(vs)`,`1`,`3`,`Specific surface area - used in dissolution`],[`Weight-moment mean, d(wm)`,`1`,`4`,`Dominated by the largest particles`]],caption:`The volume-surface mean d(vs) is the one used to calculate specific surface area, and it is the most frequently asked.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Methods of particle size determination`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`Approx. size range`,`Diameter obtained`],rows:[[`Optical microscopy`,`0.2 to 100 micrometres`,`Projected area / Feret / Martin`],[`Sieving`,`40 to 9500 micrometres`,`Sieve diameter`],[`Sedimentation (Andreasen pipette)`,`1 to 200 micrometres`,`Stokes diameter`],[`Coulter counter (electrical sensing zone)`,`0.5 to 500 micrometres`,`Volume diameter`],[`Laser light scattering / diffraction`,`0.02 to 2000 micrometres`,`Equivalent volume diameter`],[`Permeametry / gas adsorption (BET)`,`Surface area, not size directly`,`Surface-volume diameter`]],caption:`Microscopy is the only method that also reveals shape and aggregation, but it is slow and only two dimensions are seen. The Andreasen pipette applies Stokes law, so it assumes spherical particles and laminar settling.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Sieve numbers: a higher sieve number means a smaller aperture. Powder is shaken through a nest of sieves with the coarsest on top for a stated time, and each fraction is weighed.`,`The Coulter counter measures the change in electrical resistance as a particle passes through a small orifice in an electrolyte; the pulse height is proportional to particle volume.`,`Andreasen pipette: samples of the settling suspension are withdrawn from a fixed depth at fixed times and dried; Stokes law converts time to diameter.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Flow properties - the master table`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Flow character`,`Carr index (%)`,`Hausner ratio`,`Angle of repose (degrees)`],rows:[[`Excellent`,`10 or less`,`1.00 to 1.11`,`25 to 30`],[`Good`,`11 to 15`,`1.12 to 1.18`,`31 to 35`],[`Fair (aid not needed)`,`16 to 20`,`1.19 to 1.25`,`36 to 40`],[`Passable (may hang up)`,`21 to 25`,`1.26 to 1.34`,`41 to 45`],[`Poor (agitation needed)`,`26 to 31`,`1.35 to 1.45`,`46 to 55`],[`Very poor`,`32 to 37`,`1.46 to 1.59`,`56 to 65`],[`Very very poor`,`More than 38`,`More than 1.60`,`More than 66`]],caption:`The USP compendial flow scale. Carr index and Hausner ratio are both derived from the same two densities.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Carr index (%) = [ (tapped density - bulk density) / tapped density ] x 100      Hausner ratio = tapped density / bulk density      tan(theta) = h / r`,caption:`Carr compressibility index and Hausner ratio use bulk and tapped density (or, equivalently, bulk and tapped volume). In the angle of repose, h = height of the powder heap and r = radius of its base.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Which volume goes on the bottom`,text:`Carr index uses tapped density (or the tapped volume equivalent) in the denominator, not bulk. Using bulk in the denominator gives a bigger, wrong number. Hausner ratio, in contrast, puts bulk on the bottom.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Improving flow`,text:`Add a glidant (colloidal silicon dioxide, talc), granulate to make larger spherical particles, remove fines, control moisture, and use spray-dried or directly compressible excipients. Lubricants (magnesium stearate) reduce die wall friction, not flow - keep the two apart.`}]},{topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is the arithmetic: specific surface area, number of particles per gram, porosity and packing, and the statistics of a log-normal size distribution.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Sv = 6 / d(vs)      and      Sw = 6 / (rho x d(vs))`,caption:`Specific surface area for spherical particles. Sv = surface area per unit volume of particles (cm2/cm3); Sw = surface area per unit weight (cm2/g); rho = true density; d(vs) = volume-surface (Sauter) mean diameter. For non-spherical particles a shape factor is applied.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`N = 6 / (pi x rho x d(vn)^3)`,caption:`Number of particles per gram of powder. rho = true density (g/cm3); d(vn) = volume-number mean diameter (cm). It follows from the fact that one sphere has volume pi x d^3 / 6.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked density and flow example. 50 g of a granulation occupies 100 mL when poured and 80 mL after tapping.`,`Bulk density = 50 / 100 = 0.50 g/mL. Tapped density = 50 / 80 = 0.625 g/mL.`,`Carr index = (0.625 - 0.50) / 0.625 x 100 = 0.125 / 0.625 x 100 = 20 per cent - fair flow.`,`Hausner ratio = 0.625 / 0.50 = 1.25 - consistent with fair flow.`,`If the true density is 1.25 g/mL, porosity of the poured bed = 1 - (0.50 / 1.25) = 1 - 0.40 = 0.60, that is 60 per cent voids.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked specific surface example. A powder has a volume-surface mean diameter of 10 micrometres and a true density of 3.0 g/cm3.`,`Convert: 10 micrometres = 10 x 10^-4 cm = 1 x 10^-3 cm.`,`Sw = 6 / (rho x d) = 6 / (3.0 x 1 x 10^-3) = 6 / (3 x 10^-3) = 2000 cm2/g, that is 0.2 m2/g.`,`Halve the diameter to 5 micrometres and Sw doubles to 4000 cm2/g - specific surface is inversely proportional to diameter.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Packing arrangements and distributions`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Packing of uniform spheres`,`Porosity`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Closest (rhombohedral) packing`,`About 26 per cent`,`Theoretical densest`],[`Loosest (cubic) packing`,`About 48 per cent`,`Theoretical loosest`],[`Real pharmaceutical powders`,`Roughly 30 to 50 per cent`,`Irregular shapes and mixed sizes`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Most pharmaceutical powders follow a log-normal distribution: plotting cumulative percentage undersize on a probability scale against log diameter gives a straight line.`,`The geometric mean diameter is read at the 50 per cent point; the geometric standard deviation is the ratio of the 84.13 per cent size to the 50 per cent size.`,`A geometric standard deviation of 1 means a monodisperse powder; higher values mean a wider spread.`,`The Hatch-Choate equations convert one type of mean diameter to another once the geometric mean and geometric standard deviation are known.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Number versus weight distributions`,text:`A number distribution is dominated by the many small particles; a weight or volume distribution is dominated by the few large ones. The same powder therefore gives a much larger mean diameter by sieving or Coulter counter than by microscopy. Always state which distribution the mean came from.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Facts that recur`,text:`Specific surface area is inversely proportional to particle diameter. Number of particles per gram is inversely proportional to the cube of the diameter. Porosity of a powder bed is independent of particle size for identical spheres, since it depends only on the packing arrangement.`}]},{topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Chemical kinetics is the study of how fast a drug decomposes. Stability testing applies that science to predict how long a product keeps its label claim. The order of a reaction tells you what the rate depends on; the rate constant tells you how fast; the half-life and shelf life tell you what it means in practice.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Order versus molecularity`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Order of reaction is the sum of the exponents of concentration terms in the experimentally determined rate equation. It can be zero, fractional or negative and comes from experiment.`,`Molecularity is the number of molecules taking part in a single elementary step. It is always a whole positive number and comes from the mechanism.`,`For a complex reaction, order and molecularity need not be equal.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The orders you must know cold`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Order`,`Rate equation (integrated)`,`Linear plot`,`Half-life`,`Units of k`],rows:[[`Zero`,`C = C0 - k t`,`C against t`,`t(1/2) = C0 / (2 k)`,`mg mL-1 h-1 (conc/time)`],[`First`,`log C = log C0 - k t / 2.303`,`log C against t`,`t(1/2) = 0.693 / k`,`h-1 (time-1)`],[`Second (equal concentrations)`,`1/C = 1/C0 + k t`,`1/C against t`,`t(1/2) = 1 / (k C0)`,`L mol-1 h-1 (conc-1 time-1)`]],caption:`Only the first-order half-life is independent of the starting concentration. That is the single most examined line in this topic.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`t(90) for first order = 0.105 / k      t(90) for zero order = 0.1 x C0 / k`,caption:`Shelf life t(90) is the time for the drug to fall to 90 per cent of its labelled amount. For first order it comes from 2.303/k x log(100/90) = 0.105/k.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Zero order does not mean no reaction`,text:`Zero order means the rate is constant and independent of concentration, giving a straight-line fall in concentration. Suspensions of a poorly soluble drug degrade by apparent (pseudo) zero order because the dissolved concentration is held at saturation by the reservoir of undissolved solid.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`How drugs decompose`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Pathway`,`Chemistry`,`Classic examples`],rows:[[`Hydrolysis`,`Water attacks an ester, amide, lactam or imide`,`Aspirin, procaine, atropine, benzylpenicillin, chloramphenicol`],[`Oxidation`,`Free-radical chain reaction with oxygen`,`Adrenaline, ascorbic acid, morphine, vitamins A and E, phenols`],[`Photolysis`,`Light supplies the activation energy`,`Riboflavin, nifedipine, chlorpromazine, sodium nitroprusside, furosemide`],[`Others`,`Racemisation, decarboxylation, isomerisation, polymerisation`,`Adrenaline racemisation, ampicillin polymerisation`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Hydrolysis is the commonest`,text:`Hydrolysis is the most frequent degradation route for drugs in solution, and esters and amides (including the beta-lactam ring) are its usual victims. If a question gives a drug with an ester group in an aqueous solution, hydrolysis is the answer.`}]},{topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you must determine an order from data, apply the Arrhenius equation to accelerated stability data, use Q10 shortcuts, and quote the ICH conditions India works under.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Determining the order of a reaction`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Substitution method: put the data into each integrated equation in turn; the one giving a constant k is the correct order.`,`Graphical method: plot C, log C and 1/C against time; whichever gives a straight line identifies the order as zero, first or second respectively.`,`Half-life method: t(1/2) is proportional to 1 / C0 raised to (n - 1). If half-life is independent of C0 the reaction is first order; if it doubles when C0 doubles it is zero order; if it halves when C0 doubles it is second order.`,`Initial rate (differential) method: measure initial rates at different starting concentrations and take logarithms.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Temperature and the Arrhenius equation`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`k = A x e^(-Ea / RT)      log k = log A - Ea / (2.303 x R x T)`,caption:`A = frequency (Arrhenius) factor; Ea = activation energy in J/mol; R = 8.314 J/mol/K; T = absolute temperature in kelvin. A plot of log k against 1/T is a straight line of slope -Ea/(2.303 R).`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log (k2 / k1) = [ Ea / (2.303 x R) ] x [ (T2 - T1) / (T1 x T2) ]`,caption:`The two-temperature form used in accelerated stability work: measure k at high temperature, extrapolate to room temperature and hence to shelf life.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Typical activation energies for pharmaceutical degradation are 12 to 24 kcal/mol (about 50 to 100 kJ/mol).`,`A rough rule: reaction rate roughly doubles to trebles for every 10 degree rise in temperature.`,`Accelerated testing is only valid if the degradation mechanism is unchanged at the higher temperature. It fails for suspensions, emulsions, semisolids and proteins, where physical change intervenes.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Q10 = k(T + 10) / k(T)      and      t90(T2) = t90(T1) / Q10 ^ [ (T2 - T1) / 10 ]`,caption:`Q10 is the factor by which the rate constant changes for a 10 degree rise. Q10 is 2 for Ea about 12.2 kcal/mol, 3 for about 19.4 kcal/mol and 4 for about 24.5 kcal/mol. Values of 2 and 3 are the ones used in problems.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Stabilisation strategies`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Degradation`,`Stabilisation approach`],rows:[[`Hydrolysis`,`Adjust to the pH of maximum stability, buffer, reduce water (dry powder for reconstitution), use a water-miscible cosolvent of low dielectric constant, complexation, suspension of a less soluble derivative, store cold`],[`Oxidation`,`Antioxidants (sodium metabisulphite, ascorbic acid, BHA, BHT, alpha-tocopherol), chelating agents (disodium edetate) to trap trace metal catalysts, nitrogen purging, lower pH, amber glass`],[`Photolysis`,`Amber or opaque containers, secondary carton, light-resistant film coating, titanium dioxide opacifier`],[`Microbial`,`Preservatives, sterilisation, low water activity`]],caption:`Sodium metabisulphite is water-soluble and used for aqueous injections; BHA, BHT and alpha-tocopherol are oil-soluble and used in oily and semisolid products.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`ICH stability zones`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Zone`,`Climate`,`Long-term condition`],rows:[[`I`,`Temperate`,`21 C / 45 per cent RH`],[`II`,`Subtropical, Mediterranean`,`25 C / 60 per cent RH`],[`III`,`Hot and dry`,`30 C / 35 per cent RH`],[`IVA`,`Hot and humid`,`30 C / 65 per cent RH`],[`IVB`,`Hot and very humid`,`30 C / 75 per cent RH`]],caption:`India falls in Zone IVB. Long-term testing is 30 C plus or minus 2 and 75 per cent RH plus or minus 5 for 12 months; accelerated testing is 40 C plus or minus 2 and 75 per cent RH plus or minus 5 for 6 months.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Significant change on accelerated testing`,text:`A 5 per cent loss of assay from the initial value, any degradant exceeding its limit, failure of pH, dissolution or appearance specifications counts as significant change. If it occurs during accelerated testing, intermediate condition testing (30 C / 65 per cent RH) is required.`}]},{topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work is fully worked numericals plus the catalysis and solid-state detail that separates a confident answer from a guess.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked shelf-life problem. A drug in solution degrades by first order with k = 0.0005 per hour at 25 C.`,`t(1/2) = 0.693 / 0.0005 = 1386 hours, about 58 days.`,`t(90) = 0.105 / 0.0005 = 210 hours, about 8.75 days.`,`Note the ratio: t(90) is always 0.105/0.693 = 0.1515 times the half-life for a first-order reaction, whatever the value of k.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked Arrhenius problem. k at 60 C (333 K) is 0.02 per hour. Ea = 83.14 kJ/mol. Find k at 25 C (298 K).`,`log (k2/k1) = Ea/(2.303 R) x (T2 - T1)/(T1 T2). Here T1 = 298 K, T2 = 333 K.`,`Ea/(2.303 R) = 83140 / (2.303 x 8.314) = 83140 / 19.147 = 4342 K.`,`(T2 - T1)/(T1 x T2) = 35 / (298 x 333) = 35 / 99234 = 3.527 x 10^-4.`,`log (k(333)/k(298)) = 4342 x 3.527 x 10^-4 = 1.531, so the ratio = 10^1.531 = 34.0.`,`k at 25 C = 0.02 / 34.0 = 5.88 x 10^-4 per hour, giving t(90) = 0.105 / 5.88 x 10^-4 = 179 hours.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked Q10 problem. A product has a shelf life of 3 months at 40 C. Estimate the shelf life at 20 C taking Q10 = 3.`,`Temperature drops by 20 degrees, so the exponent is (20 - 40)/10 = -2.`,`t90(20 C) = t90(40 C) / 3^(-2) = 3 x 3^2 = 3 x 9 = 27 months.`,`With Q10 = 2 the same calculation gives 3 x 4 = 12 months - always state the Q10 assumed.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Catalysis and pH profiles`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Specific acid-base catalysis: the rate depends only on hydrogen ion or hydroxide ion concentration, so it changes with pH but not with buffer concentration.`,`General acid-base catalysis: the rate also depends on the concentration of the buffer species themselves. Detected by running the reaction at constant pH but increasing buffer strength.`,`A pH-rate profile plots log k against pH; the minimum identifies the pH of maximum stability. Aspirin is most stable around pH 2.5; adrenaline injections are buffered around pH 3 to 4.`,`Ionic strength affects the rate of reactions between ions (primary salt effect); it is standard practice to hold ionic strength constant when studying pH effects.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Situation`,`Apparent order`,`Reason`],rows:[[`Drug in dilute aqueous solution with excess water`,`Pseudo first order`,`Water concentration is effectively constant`],[`Drug suspension with excess undissolved solid`,`Apparent (pseudo) zero order`,`Dissolved concentration held at saturation solubility`],[`Enzyme reaction at saturating substrate`,`Zero order`,`Enzyme fully occupied, rate is Vmax`],[`Bimolecular reaction, both reactants dilute`,`Second order`,`Rate depends on both concentrations`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Solid state and complex kinetics`,text:`Solid-state decomposition often follows a sigmoidal (S-shaped) curve with an induction period, an acceleration phase and a decay phase, and is described by the Prout-Tompkins equation. It cannot be treated with simple solution kinetics.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Half-life language`,text:`For zero order, half-life falls as the drug is used up because it is proportional to C0. For first order, half-life is constant. For second order, half-life is inversely proportional to C0, so a more concentrated solution has a shorter half-life. Confusing these three costs an easy mark.`}]},{topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A buffer is a solution that resists a change in pH when a small amount of acid or alkali is added, or when it is diluted. It is made from a weak acid and its conjugate base (or a weak base and its conjugate acid). Tonicity is a separate idea: it describes how a solution behaves towards red blood cells, and it depends on the number of dissolved particles, not on pH.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`How a buffer works`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Added acid (H+) is mopped up by the salt (conjugate base) and converted to the weak acid.`,`Added alkali (OH-) is neutralised by the weak acid and converted to the salt.`,`Either way, the salt-to-acid ratio changes only slightly, so the pH barely moves.`,`Acetic acid with sodium acetate is the standard example; the bicarbonate, phosphate and protein systems are the buffers of blood.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`pH = pKa + log ( [salt] / [acid] )`,caption:`Henderson-Hasselbalch equation for an acidic buffer. [salt] = concentration of the conjugate base; [acid] = concentration of the undissociated weak acid. For a basic buffer, pH = pKw - pKb + log ( [base] / [salt] ).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`When [salt] equals [acid], the ratio is 1, log 1 = 0, and pH = pKa. This is the centre of the buffer.`,`A buffer works usefully over the range pKa plus or minus 1 pH unit, that is over salt-to-acid ratios of 10:1 to 1:10.`,`So choose a buffer whose pKa is within one unit of the pH you want.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Tonicity in plain terms`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Term`,`Compared with blood`,`Effect on red cells`],rows:[[`Isotonic`,`Same effective osmotic pressure`,`No change in cell shape or volume`],[`Hypotonic`,`Lower`,`Water enters, cell swells and bursts - haemolysis`],[`Hypertonic`,`Higher`,`Water leaves, cell shrinks - crenation`]],caption:`A 0.9 per cent w/v sodium chloride solution (normal saline) is isotonic with blood and lacrimal fluid. So is 5.0 per cent w/v dextrose.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Blood and tears have a freezing point of about -0.52 degrees Celsius, and an osmotic pressure of about 7.4 atmospheres at 37 C.`,`Any solution whose freezing point is -0.52 C is isotonic with blood.`,`Hypotonic solutions cause pain and haemolysis on injection; hypertonic ones cause pain and, in the eye, stinging.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Isotonic is not the same as isosmotic`,text:`Isosmotic means the same measured osmotic pressure as blood, a purely physical statement. Isotonic means it does not damage red cells - a biological statement that also depends on whether the solute crosses the cell membrane. A 1.9 per cent w/v urea solution is isosmotic with blood but haemolyses red cells, because urea crosses the membrane freely. Boric acid 1.9 per cent is isosmotic and haemolytic for blood but is safe for the eye.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Hypo swells, hyper shrinks`,text:`Hypotonic - too little solute outside, so water rushes in and the cell bursts (haemolysis). Hypertonic - too much solute outside, so water is pulled out and the cell shrivels (crenation).`}]},{topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The working level is buffer capacity with the Van Slyke equation, the official buffer systems, and the two classes of tonicity adjustment calculation. Numerical questions here are quick marks if the method is drilled.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Buffer capacity`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`beta = dB / d(pH)`,caption:`Buffer capacity (Van Slyke index) = gram equivalents per litre of strong base or acid (dB) needed to produce one unit change in pH (d(pH)). It is a dimensionless-looking but concentration-based measure of buffer strength; the larger the value, the more resistant the buffer.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`beta = 2.303 x C x [ Ka x (H3O+) ] / [ Ka + (H3O+) ]^2`,caption:`Van Slyke equation. C = total buffer concentration (sum of salt and acid, mol/L); Ka = dissociation constant of the weak acid; (H3O+) = hydrogen ion concentration of the buffer.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Buffer capacity is maximum when pH = pKa, that is when salt and acid are equal.`,`At that point beta(max) = 2.303 x C / 4 = 0.576 x C. Learn this constant.`,`Buffer capacity rises with the total concentration of buffer components and falls on dilution.`,`Capacity is also raised at extremes of pH by the self-buffering of hydrogen and hydroxide ions themselves.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Official and pharmaceutical buffer systems`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`System`,`Components`,`Useful pH range / use`],rows:[[`Sorensen phosphate`,`Sodium dihydrogen phosphate and disodium hydrogen phosphate`,`About 5.9 to 8.0; injections and ophthalmics`],[`Walpole acetate`,`Acetic acid and sodium acetate`,`About 3.6 to 5.6`],[`Palitzsch borate`,`Boric acid and sodium borate (borax)`,`About 6.8 to 9.1; eye drops`],[`Atkins and Pantin`,`Boric acid and sodium carbonate`,`About 7.6 to 11`],[`Gifford`,`Boric acid and sodium carbonate`,`Ophthalmic, wide range`],[`Clark and Lubs`,`HCl-KCl, phthalate, phosphate, borate series`,`pH 1.2 to 10.0 standard buffers`]],caption:`Boric acid buffers are preferred for the eye because they are well tolerated and mildly bacteriostatic; phosphate buffers can precipitate with zinc and calcium salts.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Tonicity adjustment - the two classes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Methods`,`What is added`],rows:[[`Class I`,`Cryoscopic (freezing point depression) method and sodium chloride equivalent method`,`A calculated weight of sodium chloride or dextrose is added to the drug solution`],[`Class II`,`White-Vincent method and Sprowls method`,`Water is added to make an isotonic solution, then bulk is made up with an isotonic diluting vehicle`]],caption:`Class I adds a tonicity agent to the drug; Class II dilutes the drug to isotonicity and then tops up with isotonic vehicle.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`W = (0.52 - a) / b`,caption:`Freezing point depression method. W = grams of adjusting substance per 100 mL; 0.52 = freezing point depression of blood in degrees Celsius; a = freezing point depression produced by the unadjusted drug solution; b = freezing point depression of a 1 per cent w/v solution of the adjusting substance (0.576 for sodium chloride).`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`E = 17 x Liso / M      and      V = w x E x 111.1`,caption:`E = sodium chloride equivalent, the weight of sodium chloride that produces the same osmotic effect as 1 g of the drug; Liso = the molar freezing point depression constant for that ionic class; M = molecular weight of the drug. In the White-Vincent equation V = volume in mL of isotonic solution obtainable from w grams of drug; 111.1 is the volume of isotonic solution produced by 1 g of sodium chloride.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type of electrolyte`,`Liso value`,`Example`],rows:[[`Non-electrolytes`,`1.9`,`Dextrose, urea, glycerin`],[`Weak electrolytes`,`2.0`,`Boric acid, phenobarbitone`],[`Di-divalent electrolytes`,`2.0`,`Magnesium sulphate, zinc sulphate`],[`Uni-univalent electrolytes`,`3.4`,`Sodium chloride, ephedrine hydrochloride`],[`Uni-divalent electrolytes`,`4.3`,`Sodium sulphate, atropine sulphate`],[`Di-univalent electrolytes`,`4.8`,`Calcium chloride, zinc chloride`],[`Uni-trivalent electrolytes`,`5.2`,`Sodium citrate`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Sanity check on E values`,text:`Sodium chloride itself must have E = 1. Check: E = 17 x 3.4 / 58.5 = 57.8 / 58.5 = 0.99. Most drug E values fall between 0.1 and 0.35, so a calculated value far outside that range means an arithmetic slip.`}]},{topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work here is entirely arithmetic, plus the physiological and formulation judgement about how far to buffer an ophthalmic or parenteral product.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked Henderson-Hasselbalch problem. Prepare an acetate buffer of pH 5.0 using acetic acid (pKa 4.76) and sodium acetate.`,`pH = pKa + log ([salt]/[acid]), so 5.0 = 4.76 + log ratio, giving log ratio = 0.24.`,`Ratio = antilog 0.24 = 1.74, so [salt]/[acid] = 1.74 to 1.`,`If the total buffer concentration is to be 0.2 mol/L, then acid = 0.2 / 2.74 = 0.073 mol/L and salt = 0.2 - 0.073 = 0.127 mol/L.`,`Maximum buffer capacity for this 0.2 M system would be 0.576 x 0.2 = 0.115, achieved at pH 4.76 rather than 5.0.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked freezing point (cryoscopic) problem. Make 100 mL of a 1 per cent w/v drug solution isotonic with sodium chloride. A 1 per cent w/v solution of the drug depresses the freezing point by 0.20 degrees Celsius.`,`Required total depression = 0.52 degrees; the drug already supplies a = 0.20 degrees.`,`Deficit = 0.52 - 0.20 = 0.32 degrees.`,`A 1 per cent w/v sodium chloride solution depresses the freezing point by b = 0.576 degrees.`,`W = 0.32 / 0.576 = 0.556 g of sodium chloride per 100 mL. Note the sodium chloride needed is always less than 0.9 g once the drug contributes.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked sodium chloride equivalent problem. Prepare 30 mL of 1 per cent w/v atropine sulphate eye drops isotonic with tears. E for atropine sulphate = 0.13.`,`Sodium chloride needed for 30 mL of isotonic solution = 0.9 g per 100 mL x 30 = 0.27 g.`,`Atropine sulphate present = 1 g per 100 mL x 30 mL = 0.30 g.`,`Sodium chloride equivalent contributed by the drug = 0.30 x 0.13 = 0.039 g.`,`Sodium chloride to be added = 0.27 - 0.039 = 0.231 g, that is about 0.23 g in 30 mL.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Worked White-Vincent (Class II) problem for the same drug. V = w x E x 111.1 = 0.30 x 0.13 x 111.1.`,`0.30 x 0.13 = 0.039; 0.039 x 111.1 = 4.33 mL.`,`So dissolve 0.30 g of atropine sulphate in water and make up to 4.33 mL - that volume is already isotonic.`,`Make the remaining 30 - 4.33 = 25.67 mL up with an isotonic diluting vehicle such as Sorensen phosphate buffered isotonic solution.`,`The Sprowls modification simply tabulates this V for a fixed 0.3 g of drug, removing the calculation at the counter.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Formulation judgement`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ophthalmic products should ideally be at pH 7.4, but many drugs are unstable there. The compromise is a low-capacity buffer that the tear fluid can override, avoiding prolonged stinging.`,`Tears themselves have limited buffer capacity, so a high-capacity buffer of the wrong pH will hold the eye at that pH and cause irritation.`,`Parenteral products in large volume must be isotonic; small volume injections given slowly into a large vein tolerate modest deviations.`,`Buffers also serve to keep a drug at its pH of maximum stability and to keep it in the un-ionised, absorbable form where possible.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Constants to have by heart`,text:`Freezing point of blood and tears -0.52 C; freezing point depression of 1 per cent w/v sodium chloride 0.576 C; isotonic sodium chloride 0.9 per cent w/v; isotonic dextrose 5.0 per cent w/v; 1 g of sodium chloride makes 111.1 mL of isotonic solution; beta(max) = 0.576 x C.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`When the drug is already hypertonic`,text:`If the sodium chloride equivalent contributed by the drug exceeds 0.9 g per 100 mL, no tonicity agent can be added - the solution is already hypertonic and must be diluted, or accepted as hypertonic if the route allows.`}]},{topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A complex is formed when two or more molecules join by weak, reversible forces such as coordinate bonds, hydrogen bonding, van der Waals forces or charge transfer, without making a new covalent compound. The bigger molecule is the substrate or ligand donor and the smaller one is often called the ligand. Protein binding is simply complexation in which the partner is a plasma protein.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The three families of complex`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Family`,`Nature of the bond`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Metal ion complexes`,`Coordinate bond from ligand lone pair to metal`,`Inorganic complexes, chelates (EDTA with calcium), haemoglobin, chlorophyll`],[`Organic molecular complexes`,`Hydrogen bonding, dipole and charge transfer forces`,`Caffeine with benzoic acid, quinhydrone, picric acid complexes, PVP with drugs`],[`Inclusion (occlusion) compounds`,`Physical entrapment inside a cavity or channel`,`Cyclodextrins, urea channel complexes, hydroquinone clathrates`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A chelate is a metal complex in which one ligand donates two or more pairs of electrons, forming a ring. Chelates are far more stable than simple complexes.`,`Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) is the standard chelating agent - used to trap trace metals that catalyse oxidation, and to treat lead poisoning as calcium disodium edetate.`,`Dimercaprol (BAL), penicillamine and desferrioxamine are other therapeutic chelators.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why complexation matters in a formulation`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Increases solubility of a poorly soluble drug (caffeine complexes, cyclodextrin inclusion).`,`Improves stability by shielding a labile group from water, oxygen or light.`,`Masks bitter taste and unpleasant odour.`,`Converts a liquid or volatile drug into a free-flowing powder.`,`Can also be a problem - tetracycline chelates calcium, iron and aluminium in milk and antacids and its absorption falls sharply.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Protein binding in one page`},{kind:`para`,text:`Once in plasma, a drug exists in two forms: bound to protein and free. Only the free drug can leave the capillary, act at the receptor, be metabolised or be filtered by the glomerulus. Binding is reversible, so the bound drug acts as a circulating reservoir that keeps topping up the free fraction.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Plasma protein`,`Binds mainly`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Albumin`,`Acidic drugs`,`Warfarin, phenylbutazone, aspirin, sulphonamides, phenytoin`],[`Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein`,`Basic drugs`,`Propranolol, lignocaine, imipramine, quinidine`],[`Lipoproteins`,`Lipophilic drugs and some basic drugs`,`Cyclosporine, chlorpromazine`],[`Globulins and specific carriers`,`Hormones, vitamins, metals`,`Transcortin, thyroxine binding globulin, transferrin`]],caption:`Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein and the principal drug binder.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Only free drug works`,text:`A highly bound drug has a small volume of distribution, a long half-life and slow clearance, because the bound fraction is not available for distribution, metabolism or glomerular filtration. It can, however, still be cleared by active tubular secretion, which strips drug off the protein.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Acid to albumin`,text:`Acidic drugs to Albumin (both start with A); basic drugs to alpha-1 acid Glycoprotein.`}]},{topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam-level detail is cyclodextrins, the methods used to detect a complex and measure its stability constant, and the quantitative treatment of protein binding.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cyclodextrins`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Cyclodextrin`,`Glucose units`,`Cavity diameter (approx.)`,`Water solubility at 25 C`],rows:[[`Alpha`,`6`,`4.7 to 5.3 angstrom`,`About 14.5 g/100 mL`],[`Beta`,`7`,`6.0 to 6.5 angstrom`,`About 1.85 g/100 mL`],[`Gamma`,`8`,`7.5 to 8.3 angstrom`,`About 23.2 g/100 mL`]],caption:`Beta-cyclodextrin is the least water soluble because intramolecular hydrogen bonding between the 2 and 3 hydroxyl groups locks the ring; it is nonetheless the most used because its cavity fits most drug molecules.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Cyclodextrins are cyclic oligosaccharides shaped like a truncated cone - hydrophilic outside, hydrophobic (lipophilic) cavity inside.`,`The drug sits inside the cavity as a guest; no covalent bond is made, so the complex dissociates on dilution and releases free drug.`,`Modified derivatives - hydroxypropyl beta-cyclodextrin and sulphobutyl ether beta-cyclodextrin - were developed because parenteral beta-cyclodextrin is nephrotoxic.`,`Benefits: higher solubility and dissolution rate, improved chemical and photostability, taste masking, reduced gastric irritation, liquid to powder conversion.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Methods of analysis of complexes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`What it measures`,`Note`],rows:[[`Method of continuous variation (Job plot)`,`Stoichiometry of the complex`,`Plot a property against mole fraction; the maximum gives the ratio`],[`Phase solubility (Higuchi and Connors)`,`Stability constant and complex type`,`Plot total drug solubility against ligand concentration`],[`Distribution (partition) method`,`Stability constant`,`Classic for the iodine-potassium iodide system`],[`pH titration / potentiometry`,`Stability constant of metal chelates`,`Bjerrum method`],[`Spectroscopy (UV, IR, NMR)`,`Evidence and site of complexation`,`Shift or new charge transfer band`],[`Equilibrium dialysis and ultrafiltration`,`Free versus bound fraction`,`Standard for protein binding`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`K = slope / [ S0 x (1 - slope) ]`,caption:`Stability (formation) constant of a 1:1 complex from a phase-solubility diagram. slope = gradient of the initial linear portion of total drug solubility against ligand concentration; S0 = intrinsic solubility of the drug in the absence of ligand. A larger K means a more stable complex.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Phase-solubility diagrams are classified as type A (soluble complexes: AL linear, AP positive deviation, AN negative deviation) and type B (limited solubility complexes: BS soluble to a limit, BI insoluble).`,`A slope greater than 1 in a phase-solubility plot means the complex is not 1:1.`,`Beta-cyclodextrin usually gives type B curves because the complex itself has limited solubility; the hydroxypropyl derivative gives type AL curves.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Quantifying protein binding`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`r = (nu x K x [Df]) / (1 + K x [Df])      Klotz:  1/r = 1 / (nu x K x [Df]) + 1/nu`,caption:`r = moles of drug bound per mole of protein; nu = number of binding sites per protein molecule; K = association (binding) constant; [Df] = free drug concentration. The Klotz reciprocal plot of 1/r against 1/[Df] gives intercept 1/nu and slope 1/(nu x K).`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Scatchard:  r / [Df] = nu x K - r x K`,caption:`Scatchard plot of r/[Df] against r: slope = -K, y-intercept = nu x K, and the x-intercept gives nu directly. A curved Scatchard plot indicates more than one class of binding site.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Equilibrium dialysis in one line`,text:`Plasma (or protein solution) is placed inside a semipermeable membrane bag and buffer outside; free drug crosses until equilibrium, bound drug cannot. The concentration outside is the free concentration, and inside minus outside gives the bound. Watch for the Donnan effect and for drug adsorption to the membrane.`}]},{topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work joins the physical chemistry to clinical consequence: what happens when one drug displaces another from albumin, and when that actually matters.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Displacement interactions`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Displaced drug`,`Displacer`,`Clinical result`],rows:[[`Warfarin (99 per cent bound)`,`Phenylbutazone, aspirin, sulphonamides`,`Sudden rise in free warfarin, bleeding`],[`Phenytoin`,`Valproate, salicylates`,`Toxicity at an apparently normal total plasma level`],[`Methotrexate`,`Salicylates, sulphonamides`,`Marrow suppression`],[`Bilirubin (endogenous)`,`Sulphonamides, vitamin K in neonates`,`Kernicterus in the newborn`],[`Tolbutamide`,`Sulphonamides, salicylates`,`Hypoglycaemia`]],caption:`Displacement is clinically significant only for drugs that are highly bound (above about 90 per cent), have a small volume of distribution and a narrow therapeutic index.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Why a small change in binding matters. A drug is 99 per cent bound, so the free fraction is 1 per cent.`,`If a displacer reduces binding to 98 per cent, the free fraction becomes 2 per cent.`,`Free drug concentration has therefore doubled - a 100 per cent rise - from a change of only one percentage point in binding.`,`For a drug that is 50 per cent bound, the same one-point change moves the free fraction from 50 to 51 per cent, a rise of only 2 per cent - clinically irrelevant.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The rise in free drug is usually transient: more free drug means more distribution and more clearance, so a new steady state is reached with a lower total but a similar free concentration.`,`Danger arises when displacement is combined with inhibition of metabolism - phenylbutazone with warfarin does both.`,`Therapeutic drug monitoring reports total plasma concentration, so in hypoalbuminaemia or displacement the reported total underestimates the free (active) level, notably for phenytoin.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Factors that alter protein binding`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Drug factors: physicochemical nature, total concentration (binding saturates at high doses, as with phenytoin and salicylates).`,`Protein factors: concentration (hypoalbuminaemia in nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, burns, pregnancy and old age), and the presence of modified albumin in uraemia.`,`Physiological: neonates have low albumin and high free fatty acids, so free fractions are higher; alpha-1 acid glycoprotein rises in inflammation and lowers the free fraction of basic drugs.`,`Drug interactions: competitive displacement at the same site, or allosteric change at a different site.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Albumin binding site`,`Also called`,`Typical ligands`],rows:[[`Site I`,`Warfarin - azapropazone site`,`Warfarin, phenylbutazone, valproate, sulphonamides`],[`Site II`,`Indole - benzodiazepine site`,`Diazepam, ibuprofen, naproxen, tryptophan`]],caption:`Two drugs competing for the same site displace each other; drugs at different sites usually do not.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Tissue binding is not protein binding`,text:`Extensive plasma protein binding keeps the drug in the plasma and gives a small volume of distribution (warfarin, about 8 L). Extensive tissue binding pulls the drug out of plasma and gives a very large volume of distribution (digoxin about 500 L, chloroquine over 10,000 L). Do not use one to explain the other.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Complexation numbers`,text:`Higher stability constant means more of the drug is tied up, so complexation for solubility enhancement needs a moderate K - too high and the drug is not released at the absorption site. Iodine with polyvinylpyrrolidone (povidone iodine) is the classic example of a complex used deliberately to hold and slowly release an active.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-pp-solubility-1`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`One gram of a drug dissolves in 45 mL of water. According to the IP and USP descriptive terms, the drug is:`,options:[`Soluble`,`Freely soluble`,`Sparingly soluble`,`Slightly soluble`],answer:2,explanation:`Sparingly soluble covers 30 to 100 parts of solvent for 1 part of solute, and 45 parts falls in that band. Soluble is the tempting distractor but it stops at 30 parts; slightly soluble does not begin until 100 parts.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-2`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Micronising a poorly soluble drug increases its dissolution rate mainly by changing which term of the Noyes-Whitney equation?`,options:[`A, the effective surface area`,`D, the diffusion coefficient`,`h, the diffusion layer thickness`,`Cs, the saturation solubility`],answer:0,explanation:`Breaking one particle into many multiplies the exposed surface area A, and dissolution rate is directly proportional to A. Cs is the attractive distractor, but equilibrium solubility only changes appreciably below about 1 to 2 micrometres, whereas the surface area gain is immediate and large.`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-3`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following solvents would be classed as semipolar and used as an intermediate solvent to blend water with a non-polar liquid?`,options:[`Benzene (dielectric constant about 2.3)`,`Chloroform (about 5)`,`Ethanol (about 25)`,`Water (about 80)`],answer:2,explanation:`Semipolar solvents have intermediate dielectric constants, roughly 20 to 50, and ethanol at about 25 is the classic example; they induce some polarity in non-polar molecules and act as intermediate solvents. Chloroform at about 5 is non-polar and cannot bridge the two, so it is the wrong choice.`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-4`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A solution contains 0.060 mol/L of the sodium salt of a weak acid whose pKa is 4.2. The intrinsic solubility of the free acid is 0.006 mol/L. Below what pH will the free acid precipitate?`,options:[`pH 3.25`,`pH 4.20`,`pH 5.15`,`pH 6.30`],answer:2,explanation:`pHp = pKa + log[(S - S0)/S0] = 4.2 + log[(0.060 - 0.006)/0.006] = 4.2 + log(0.054/0.006) = 4.2 + log 9 = 4.2 + 0.954 = 5.15. Choosing pH 4.2 is the common error of forgetting the concentration term, which only applies when S - S0 equals S0.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-5`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A cream contains 20 per cent oil and 80 per cent water. A preservative with an oil/water partition coefficient of 4 is added at a total concentration of 0.4 per cent w/v. What is its concentration in the aqueous phase?`,options:[`0.10 per cent w/v`,`0.40 per cent w/v`,`0.32 per cent w/v`,`0.25 per cent w/v`],answer:3,explanation:`Cw = C(total) / (Ko/w x fraction oil + fraction water) = 0.4 / (4 x 0.2 + 0.8) = 0.4 / (0.8 + 0.8) = 0.4 / 1.6 = 0.25 per cent w/v. Answering 0.40 assumes the preservative stays entirely in water, which is exactly the mistake that leads to preservative failure in high-oil creams.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-6`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Chloramphenicol palmitate is orally effective only as one of its solid forms. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`The stable crystalline form A is absorbed; the metastable form B is inactive`,`The metastable form B is absorbed; the stable form A is essentially inactive orally`,`Both forms are equally absorbed because they are chemically identical`,`Only the anhydrous form is absorbed; both polymorphs are inactive`],answer:1,explanation:`The metastable polymorph B has a higher apparent solubility and dissolution rate and is the therapeutically useful form; stable form A dissolves too slowly to give useful blood levels. Chemical identity does not mean equal bioavailability, because dissolution rate, not chemistry, is rate limiting here.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-7`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`From the ideal solubility (van’t Hoff) equation, a solid drug will have the highest ideal solubility when it has:`,options:[`A low melting point and a low heat of fusion`,`A high melting point and a low heat of fusion`,`A low melting point and a high heat of fusion`,`A high melting point and a high heat of fusion`],answer:0,explanation:`Ideal solubility rises as both the melting point and the heat of fusion fall, because both reflect a weak crystal lattice that is easy to break. A high melting point with a low heat of fusion is the seductive distractor, but the melting-point term dominates and a high melting solid is always harder to dissolve.`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-8`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The apparent partition coefficient of benzoic acid between benzene and water rises as the total concentration rises. The correct explanation is that benzoic acid:`,options:[`Ionises in the aqueous phase, lowering the aqueous concentration`,`Is adsorbed at the benzene-water interface`,`Dimerises in the benzene phase, raising the measured oil-phase concentration`,`Undergoes hydrolysis in water at higher concentrations`],answer:2,explanation:`Benzoic acid associates into dimers in the non-polar benzene phase, so the total concentration measured in benzene exceeds the monomer concentration and the apparent partition coefficient drifts upward with concentration. Ionisation in water does occur but it would lower, not raise, the apparent coefficient, and it does not vary in this direction with total concentration.`},{id:`q-pp-surface-1`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The surface tension of water at 20 degrees Celsius is approximately:`,options:[`28.9 dyne/cm`,`35.0 dyne/cm`,`72.8 dyne/cm`,`476 dyne/cm`],answer:2,explanation:`Water has a surface tension of about 72.8 dyne/cm (mN/m) at 20 C, unusually high because of hydrogen bonding. 35.0 dyne/cm is the water-benzene interfacial tension and 476 dyne/cm belongs to mercury, both classic decoys in this question.`},{id:`q-pp-surface-2`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which change does NOT occur as a surfactant solution passes through its critical micelle concentration?`,options:[`Surface tension stops falling and plateaus`,`Turbidity and light scattering increase`,`Solubilisation of water-insoluble material begins`,`Monomer concentration in the bulk rises steeply`],answer:3,explanation:`Above the CMC, additional surfactant is taken up into new micelles, so the free monomer concentration stays essentially constant rather than rising steeply. All the other three are standard signatures used to locate the CMC experimentally.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-surface-3`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which method CANNOT be used to measure the interfacial tension between two immiscible liquids?`,options:[`Capillary rise method`,`Drop weight method`,`du Nouy ring tensiometer`,`Wilhelmy plate method`],answer:0,explanation:`Capillary rise is restricted to liquid-air surface tension and requires a zero contact angle, so it cannot handle a liquid-liquid interface. The drop weight, du Nouy ring and Wilhelmy plate methods all work at both surfaces and interfaces, which is why they are preferred for emulsion work.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-surface-4`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A blend is made from 60 per cent Tween 80 (HLB 15.0) and 40 per cent Span 80 (HLB 4.3). The HLB of the mixture is:`,options:[`10.72`,`9.65`,`11.40`,`12.05`],answer:0,explanation:`HLB(blend) = 0.6 x 15.0 + 0.4 x 4.3 = 9.00 + 1.72 = 10.72. Answering 9.65 comes from taking the simple average of 15.0 and 4.3, which ignores the weight fractions.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-surface-5`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An oil phase has a required HLB of 12. Using Tween 80 (HLB 15.0) and Span 80 (HLB 4.3), what percentage of Tween 80 is needed in the emulsifier blend?`,options:[`About 42 per cent`,`About 58 per cent`,`About 72 per cent`,`About 85 per cent`],answer:2,explanation:`Fraction of Tween 80 = (12 - 4.3) / (15.0 - 4.3) = 7.7 / 10.7 = 0.72, that is about 72 per cent Tween 80 and 28 per cent Span 80. Answering 28 per cent (or the mirror value) means the numerator was built from the high-HLB surfactant instead of the low-HLB one.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-surface-6`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A plot of C/y against C for the adsorption of a drug on charcoal gives a straight line. This indicates which isotherm, and what does the reciprocal of the slope give?`,options:[`Freundlich isotherm; the constant k`,`BET isotherm; the multilayer volume Vm`,`Freundlich isotherm; the exponent 1/n`,`Langmuir isotherm; the monolayer capacity a`],answer:3,explanation:`The Langmuir linear form is C/y = 1/(ab) + C/a, so a plot of C/y against C has slope 1/a and the reciprocal of the slope gives the monolayer capacity a. The Freundlich linear form is log(x/m) against log C, a different plot altogether, so options citing Freundlich are ruled out by the axes alone.`},{id:`q-pp-surface-7`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Sodium chloride raises the surface tension of water slightly as its concentration increases. According to the Gibbs adsorption equation, the surface excess concentration of sodium chloride is:`,options:[`Positive, because d(gamma)/dC is positive`,`Negative, because d(gamma)/dC is positive`,`Positive, because d(gamma)/dC is negative`,`Zero, because electrolytes are not surface active`],answer:1,explanation:`Gibbs gives Gamma = -(C/RT) x d(gamma)/dC. If surface tension rises with concentration then d(gamma)/dC is positive and the minus sign makes Gamma negative, that is negative adsorption with the ions driven into the bulk. It is not zero: the effect is real and measurable, just opposite in sign to that of a surfactant.`},{id:`q-pp-surface-8`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Oleic acid has a surface tension of 32.5 dyne/cm and its interfacial tension against water is 15.6 dyne/cm. Water has a surface tension of 72.8 dyne/cm. The initial spreading coefficient of oleic acid on water is:`,options:[`-24.7 dyne/cm, it will not spread`,`+24.7 dyne/cm, it will spread`,`+56.9 dyne/cm, it will spread`,`+40.3 dyne/cm, it will spread`],answer:1,explanation:`S = gamma(water) - [gamma(oleic acid) + gamma(interfacial)] = 72.8 - (32.5 + 15.6) = 72.8 - 48.1 = +24.7 dyne/cm, and a positive value means spontaneous spreading. Getting -24.7 means the subtraction was performed the wrong way round; 56.9 comes from omitting the interfacial term.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-1`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The scattering of a beam of light by a colloidal dispersion, making the beam visible as a luminous cone, is called:`,options:[`Brownian motion`,`Tyndall effect`,`Dorn effect`,`Electroosmosis`],answer:1,explanation:`The Tyndall effect is the optical property of colloids: particles of 1 nm to 0.5 micrometre scatter light, whereas a true solution does not. Brownian motion is the kinetic property (random particle movement), and the Dorn effect is the sedimentation potential, an electrical property.`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-2`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`According to Stokes law, if the particle diameter of a suspension is halved and everything else is unchanged, the sedimentation velocity becomes:`,options:[`One quarter`,`One half`,`Double`,`Unchanged`],answer:0,explanation:`Sedimentation velocity is proportional to the square of the diameter, so halving d multiplies the velocity by (1/2) squared, that is one quarter. Answering one half is the standard slip of treating the relationship as linear.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-3`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An emulsion is tested with the oil-soluble dye Sudan III and the whole field appears uniformly red under the microscope. The emulsion is:`,options:[`Oil in water`,`A microemulsion`,`A multiple w/o/w emulsion`,`Water in oil`],answer:3,explanation:`An oil-soluble dye colours the continuous phase uniformly only when oil is the continuous phase, that is a water-in-oil emulsion. In an O/W emulsion the same dye would appear as scattered red globules against a colourless background, which is the distractor most students pick.`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-4`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which set of properties describes a deflocculated suspension?`,options:[`Rapid settling, clear supernatant, loose sediment, easy redispersion`,`Slow settling, cloudy supernatant, hard caked sediment, difficult redispersion`,`Rapid settling, cloudy supernatant, hard sediment, easy redispersion`,`Slow settling, clear supernatant, loose sediment, difficult redispersion`],answer:1,explanation:`Deflocculated particles settle individually and slowly, leave the supernatant cloudy for a long time, and pack into a dense cake that is very hard to redisperse. Option one describes the flocculated system, which is the mirror image on every count.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-5`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`60 mL of a suspension in a 100 mL cylinder settles to a sediment of 30 mL. The same suspension when completely deflocculated gives a sediment of 12 mL. What are the sedimentation volume F and the degree of flocculation beta?`,options:[`F = 0.50 and beta = 2.5`,`F = 0.30 and beta = 2.5`,`F = 0.50 and beta = 0.4`,`F = 0.30 and beta = 0.4`],answer:0,explanation:`F = Vu/Vo = 30/60 = 0.50 (the original volume of suspension is 60 mL, not the cylinder capacity). F(infinity) = 12/60 = 0.20, so beta = 0.50/0.20 = 2.5. Using 100 mL as Vo gives the wrong F of 0.30, and inverting the beta ratio gives 0.4 - beta for a flocculated system is always greater than 1.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-6`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In DLVO theory, a loosely flocculated suspension that redisperses easily on shaking corresponds to particles held in the:`,options:[`Primary maximum`,`Primary minimum`,`Secondary minimum`,`Region of zero net potential energy`],answer:2,explanation:`The secondary minimum is a shallow energy well at moderate separation, so the flocs are loose and thermal or mechanical energy breaks them apart easily. The primary minimum is deep and close-range and produces irreversible caking, while the primary maximum is the energy barrier that keeps particles apart, not a state they sit in.`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-7`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Particles of diameter 20 micrometres and density 2.0 g/cm3 settle in a vehicle of density 1.0 g/cm3 and viscosity 0.01 poise. The sedimentation velocity is approximately:`,options:[`0.0022 cm/s`,`2.18 cm/s`,`0.218 cm/s`,`0.0218 cm/s`],answer:3,explanation:`d = 20 micrometres = 2 x 10^-3 cm, so d squared = 4 x 10^-6 cm2. v = [4 x 10^-6 x (2.0 - 1.0) x 981] / (18 x 0.01) = 3.924 x 10^-3 / 0.18 = 0.0218 cm/s. The commonest error is leaving the diameter in micrometres, which shifts the answer by orders of magnitude.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-8`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Adding calcium chloride to an oil-in-water emulsion stabilised with sodium stearate causes phase inversion to water-in-oil. The reason is that:`,options:[`Calcium ions raise the ionic strength and compress the electrical double layer only`,`The phase volume ratio is driven beyond 74 per cent by the added salt`,`Calcium stearate is formed, which is a lipophilic W/O emulsifying agent`,`Calcium chloride lowers the interfacial tension below the critical value`],answer:2,explanation:`The divalent calcium ion converts the monovalent sodium soap into calcium stearate, a low-HLB lipophilic soap that stabilises a water-in-oil emulsion, so the emulsion inverts. Double-layer compression alone would cause flocculation or cracking, not a change of emulsion type, and no salt addition can push the phase volume ratio past 74 per cent.`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-1`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following behaves as a Newtonian liquid?`,options:[`Bentonite magma`,`Glycerin`,`Zinc oxide paste`,`Methylcellulose gel`],answer:1,explanation:`Glycerin is a simple liquid whose viscosity is constant at all shear rates, which is the definition of Newtonian behaviour. Bentonite magma is thixotropic and plastic, zinc oxide paste is plastic, and methylcellulose gel is pseudoplastic - all structured, all non-Newtonian.`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-2`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A yield value is a characteristic feature of which type of flow?`,options:[`Newtonian`,`Pseudoplastic`,`Plastic (Bingham)`,`Dilatant`],answer:2,explanation:`Plastic or Bingham bodies do not flow until the applied shear stress exceeds the yield value, so their rheogram meets the shear stress axis above the origin. Pseudoplastic and dilatant curves both pass through the origin and therefore have no yield value, even though pseudoplastic systems also thin on shearing.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-3`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A suspension containing more than 50 per cent of deflocculated solids sets to a firm mass when stirred rapidly but flows freely when left alone. This behaviour is:`,options:[`Dilatant`,`Pseudoplastic`,`Thixotropic`,`Plastic`],answer:0,explanation:`Dilatant systems are shear thickening: at high shear rate the ordered close packing is disrupted, the voids increase and there is insufficient vehicle to lubricate the particles, so viscosity rises. Thixotropy is the opposite in outcome (structure breaks down with time of shearing) and is a time-dependent, not shear-rate-dependent, phenomenon.`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-4`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A hysteresis loop in which the material is thinner on the return, so the down-curve shows a lower consistency than the up-curve, indicates:`,options:[`Antithixotropy`,`A Newtonian system`,`Dilatancy`,`Thixotropy`],answer:3,explanation:`In thixotropy the structure is broken down during shearing and rebuilds only slowly, so on the return the material is less consistent than it was going up. Antithixotropy (negative thixotropy) gives the opposite loop because viscosity increases with time of shearing. Note the description is deliberately given in terms of consistency rather than "left" or "right": Martin plots shear stress on the x-axis while most modern rheometers plot shear rate there, so the loop appears mirrored between the two conventions and the direction alone is not a safe cue.`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-5`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which viscometer is best suited to characterising the thixotropy of an ointment?`,options:[`Ostwald U-tube viscometer`,`Falling sphere (Hoeppler) viscometer`,`Cone and plate (Ferranti-Shirley) viscometer`,`Capillary viscometer with a pressure head`],answer:2,explanation:`The cone and plate viscometer gives a constant shear rate throughout the sample, needs only a tiny sample and can be programmed up and down in shear rate, so it maps hysteresis loops precisely. Ostwald, falling sphere and capillary instruments are all single-point devices restricted to Newtonian liquids.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-6`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In an Ostwald viscometer water (density 1.00 g/cm3, viscosity 1.0 centipoise) takes 30 s to flow and a test liquid of density 1.20 g/cm3 takes 75 s. The viscosity of the test liquid is:`,options:[`3.0 centipoise`,`2.5 centipoise`,`3.6 centipoise`,`2.0 centipoise`],answer:0,explanation:`eta1/eta2 = (rho1 x t1)/(rho2 x t2), so eta1 = 1.0 x (1.20 x 75)/(1.00 x 30) = 90/30 = 3.0 centipoise. Answering 2.5 comes from using only the time ratio and forgetting the densities, which is exactly the term the Ostwald method requires because flow is driven by the liquid own head.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-7`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A liquid has a dynamic viscosity of 0.6 Pa.s and a density of 1.2 g/cm3. Its kinematic viscosity is:`,options:[`0.5 stokes`,`5 stokes`,`50 stokes`,`0.05 stokes`],answer:1,explanation:`First convert: 1 Pa.s = 10 poise, so 0.6 Pa.s = 6 poise. Kinematic viscosity = viscosity in poise divided by density in g/cm3 = 6 / 1.2 = 5 stokes (500 centistokes). Dividing 0.6 by 1.2 without the Pa.s to poise conversion gives the wrong 0.5 stokes.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-8`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`When a plastic material is measured in a cup and bob viscometer, material near the bob is sheared while material further away moves as an unyielded solid mass. This artefact is called:`,options:[`Slippage`,`Shear banding of the second kind`,`Wall depletion`,`Plug flow`],answer:3,explanation:`Plug flow occurs when the shear stress in the outer part of the annular gap falls below the yield value, so that region moves as a plug and the apparent yield value is falsely low. Cone and plate geometry avoids it because the shear rate is uniform across the whole sample.`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-1`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`True (absolute) density of a powder is best determined using a:`,options:[`Mercury displacement pycnometer`,`Helium pycnometer`,`Tapped density apparatus`,`Andreasen pipette`],answer:1,explanation:`Helium is a very small, inert molecule that penetrates even the finest pores, so a helium pycnometer measures the volume of the solid material alone and gives true density. Mercury does not enter fine pores at ordinary pressure, so a mercury pycnometer gives granule density, which is the classic distractor here.`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-2`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A granulation has a Hausner ratio of 1.10 and an angle of repose of 27 degrees. Its flow is best described as:`,options:[`Excellent`,`Fair`,`Passable`,`Very poor`],answer:0,explanation:`A Hausner ratio of 1.00 to 1.11 with an angle of repose of 25 to 30 degrees is the excellent band of the compendial flow scale. Fair flow would need a Hausner ratio around 1.19 to 1.25 and an angle of repose of 36 to 40 degrees.`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-3`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Andreasen pipette method of particle size analysis gives which type of diameter, and over what approximate size range?`,options:[`Volume diameter, 0.5 to 500 micrometres`,`Projected area diameter, 0.2 to 100 micrometres`,`Stokes diameter, 1 to 200 micrometres`,`Sieve diameter, 40 to 9500 micrometres`],answer:2,explanation:`The Andreasen pipette is a sedimentation method, so it applies Stokes law and reports the Stokes (equivalent settling) diameter over roughly 1 to 200 micrometres. Volume diameter belongs to the Coulter counter and projected area diameter to microscopy, which are different principles altogether.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-4`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`50 g of a granulation occupies 100 mL when poured and 80 mL after tapping. The Carr compressibility index is:`,options:[`20 per cent`,`16 per cent`,`25 per cent`,`80 per cent`],answer:0,explanation:`Bulk density = 50/100 = 0.50 g/mL and tapped density = 50/80 = 0.625 g/mL. Carr index = (0.625 - 0.50)/0.625 x 100 = 0.125/0.625 x 100 = 20 per cent, that is fair flow. Answering 25 per cent comes from dividing by the bulk density instead of the tapped density.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-5`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A powder has a bulk density of 0.50 g/mL and a true density of 1.25 g/mL. The porosity of the poured bed is:`,options:[`40 per cent`,`50 per cent`,`60 per cent`,`75 per cent`],answer:2,explanation:`Porosity = 1 - (bulk density / true density) = 1 - (0.50/1.25) = 1 - 0.40 = 0.60, that is 60 per cent void space. Answering 40 per cent is the ratio itself, which is the fraction occupied by solid, not the void fraction.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-6`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Coulter counter sizes particles by measuring:`,options:[`The time taken for particles to settle through a known depth`,`The angle through which a laser beam is diffracted`,`The pressure drop across a packed bed of the powder`,`The change in electrical resistance as a particle passes through a small orifice`],answer:3,explanation:`In the electrical sensing zone (Coulter) principle, particles suspended in an electrolyte pass through a narrow orifice and momentarily displace electrolyte, producing a voltage pulse proportional to particle volume. Settling time is the sedimentation principle, laser diffraction is a separate technique, and pressure drop across a bed is permeametry for surface area.`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-7`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A spherical powder has a volume-surface mean diameter of 10 micrometres and a true density of 3.0 g/cm3. Its specific surface area per gram is:`,options:[`200 cm2/g`,`600 cm2/g`,`2000 cm2/g`,`6000 cm2/g`],answer:2,explanation:`Sw = 6/(rho x d(vs)). Converting the diameter, 10 micrometres = 1 x 10^-3 cm, so Sw = 6/(3.0 x 1 x 10^-3) = 6/(3 x 10^-3) = 2000 cm2/g, that is 0.2 m2/g. Answering 6000 cm2/g comes from omitting the density term.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-8`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`For the same powder sample, a number distribution obtained by microscopy gives a much smaller mean diameter than a weight distribution obtained by sieving. The reason is that:`,options:[`Microscopy always underestimates because only two dimensions are seen`,`A number distribution is dominated by the many small particles, a weight distribution by the few large ones`,`Sieving measures agglomerates rather than primary particles`,`The two methods measure diameters at different temperatures`],answer:1,explanation:`Weighting by mass gives enormous influence to large particles because mass goes as the cube of diameter, while a number count treats a 1 micrometre and a 100 micrometre particle equally. The two-dimensional limitation of microscopy is real but it is a minor bias compared with the weighting effect, which can shift the mean several-fold.`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-1`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which statement about half-life is correct?`,options:[`Zero-order half-life is independent of initial concentration`,`First-order half-life is independent of initial concentration`,`Second-order half-life is independent of initial concentration`,`All three half-lives are independent of initial concentration`],answer:1,explanation:`For first order, t(1/2) = 0.693/k, which contains no concentration term. Zero-order half-life is C0/2k, so it rises with initial concentration, and second-order half-life is 1/(k C0), so it falls as concentration rises.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-2`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A drug suspension containing a large excess of undissolved solid degrades in solution by first order. The overall observed degradation of the suspension follows:`,options:[`Apparent (pseudo) zero order`,`True first order`,`Second order`,`Third order`],answer:0,explanation:`The reservoir of undissolved solid keeps the dissolved concentration at saturation, so the reacting concentration is constant and the loss of drug per unit time is constant - apparent zero order. It remains first order in the dissolved phase, but what the assay sees is a straight-line fall in total drug.`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-3`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The units of the rate constant for a zero-order reaction are:`,options:[`h-1`,`mL mg-1 h-1`,`L mol-1 h-1`,`mg mL-1 h-1`],answer:3,explanation:`Zero-order rate equals k, so k must carry the units of concentration divided by time, for example mg/mL per hour. Reciprocal time (h-1) belongs to first order and L mol-1 h-1 to second order - the units alone can identify the order in an exam.`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-4`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug in solution degrades by first order with k = 0.0005 per hour. Its shelf life t(90) is approximately:`,options:[`210 hours`,`139 hours`,`1386 hours`,`2100 hours`],answer:0,explanation:`t(90) = 0.105/k = 0.105/0.0005 = 210 hours, about 8.75 days. The value 1386 hours is the half-life (0.693/0.0005), the most tempting distractor because both come from the same k.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-5`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A product has a shelf life of 3 months at 40 degrees Celsius. Taking Q10 as 3, the estimated shelf life at 20 degrees Celsius is:`,options:[`9 months`,`12 months`,`18 months`,`27 months`],answer:3,explanation:`The temperature falls by 20 degrees, so the shelf life is multiplied by Q10 raised to (40 - 20)/10 = 2, that is 3 squared = 9. Shelf life = 3 x 9 = 27 months. Answering 9 months applies the factor only once, and 12 months is the answer you would get with Q10 = 2.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-6`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`What long-term stability testing condition does India currently apply, and which climatic zone does it correspond to?`,options:[`Zone II; 25 C and 60 per cent RH`,`Zone III; 30 C and 35 per cent RH`,`Zone IVA; 30 C and 65 per cent RH`,`Zone IVB; 30 C and 75 per cent RH`],answer:3,explanation:`India applies Zone IVB (hot and very humid) conditions, so long-term testing is 30 C plus or minus 2 with 75 per cent RH plus or minus 5. Zone IVA at 65 per cent RH is the near-miss, and it is a genuine one: older texts and the pre-2005 WHO map placed India in Zone IVA, so a dated source will tell you 65 per cent. Accelerated testing for both is 40 C and 75 per cent RH for six months.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-7`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A degradation has an activation energy of 83.14 kJ/mol and a rate constant of 0.02 per hour at 60 degrees Celsius (333 K). Using R = 8.314 J/mol/K, the rate constant at 25 degrees Celsius (298 K) is approximately:`,options:[`5.9 x 10^-4 per hour`,`5.9 x 10^-3 per hour`,`3.4 x 10^-2 per hour`,`6.8 x 10^-1 per hour`],answer:0,explanation:`Ea/(2.303 R) = 83140/19.147 = 4342 K, and (T2 - T1)/(T1 T2) = 35/(298 x 333) = 3.527 x 10^-4. So log(k333/k298) = 4342 x 3.527 x 10^-4 = 1.531, giving a ratio of 34. Therefore k298 = 0.02/34 = 5.9 x 10^-4 per hour. Multiplying instead of dividing by 34 gives the physically absurd answer of a faster reaction at the lower temperature.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-8`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A hydrolysis is studied at constant pH and constant ionic strength while the total acetate buffer concentration is increased. The rate constant rises. This demonstrates:`,options:[`Specific acid catalysis`,`Specific base catalysis`,`General acid-base catalysis`,`A primary salt effect`],answer:2,explanation:`If pH is held constant then hydrogen and hydroxide ion concentrations are fixed, so an increase in rate with buffer concentration must come from the buffer species themselves acting as catalysts - general acid-base catalysis. A primary salt effect is excluded because ionic strength was deliberately kept constant.`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-1`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`In an acetate buffer the concentration of sodium acetate equals the concentration of acetic acid. The pH of the buffer is:`,options:[`Equal to pKw - pKa`,`Equal to pKa`,`One unit above pKa`,`One unit below pKa`],answer:1,explanation:`Henderson-Hasselbalch gives pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]); when the two are equal the ratio is 1 and log 1 = 0, so pH = pKa. A ratio of 10:1 would move the pH one unit above pKa, which is the upper edge of the useful buffer range.`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-2`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Injecting a markedly hypotonic solution into a vein causes:`,options:[`Crenation of red cells`,`Haemolysis of red cells`,`No change in red cells`,`Precipitation of plasma proteins`],answer:1,explanation:`A hypotonic solution has a lower effective osmotic pressure than blood, so water moves into the red cells, which swell and burst - haemolysis. Crenation (shrinkage) is what a hypertonic solution causes, and it is the reverse movement of water.`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-3`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The maximum buffer capacity of a buffer whose total concentration is 0.20 mol/L is:`,options:[`0.115`,`0.058`,`0.200`,`0.460`],answer:0,explanation:`Maximum buffer capacity occurs at pH = pKa and equals 2.303 x C/4 = 0.576 x C = 0.576 x 0.20 = 0.115. Answering 0.200 confuses buffer capacity with the total buffer concentration itself, which is only one of the two factors involved.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-4`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`What salt-to-acid ratio is required to prepare an acetate buffer of pH 5.76 from acetic acid of pKa 4.76?`,options:[`1 to 10`,`1 to 1`,`10 to 1`,`100 to 1`],answer:2,explanation:`pH - pKa = 5.76 - 4.76 = 1.00, so log([salt]/[acid]) = 1 and the ratio is antilog 1 = 10, that is 10 parts salt to 1 part acid. A 1 to 10 ratio would give pH 3.76, one unit below the pKa - the direction error is the commonest slip here.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-5`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`How much sodium chloride must be added to prepare 30 mL of a 1 per cent w/v atropine sulphate eye drop isotonic with tears? (E value of atropine sulphate = 0.13)`,options:[`0.039 g`,`0.309 g`,`0.270 g`,`0.231 g`],answer:3,explanation:`Sodium chloride needed for 30 mL of isotonic solution = 0.9/100 x 30 = 0.27 g. The atropine sulphate present is 0.30 g, contributing 0.30 x 0.13 = 0.039 g of sodium chloride equivalent. Therefore sodium chloride to be added = 0.27 - 0.039 = 0.231 g. Answering 0.27 g forgets the drug contribution entirely.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-6`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 1.9 per cent w/v urea solution is isosmotic with blood, yet it haemolyses red blood cells. The reason is that:`,options:[`Urea lowers the pH of blood sharply`,`Urea crosses the red cell membrane freely, so it exerts no effective osmotic pressure across it`,`Urea binds to haemoglobin and denatures it`,`The measured freezing point depression of urea solutions is unreliable`],answer:1,explanation:`Tonicity depends only on solutes that cannot cross the membrane; urea penetrates the red cell freely, so it contributes nothing to the effective (tonic) osmotic pressure and water follows into the cell. This is precisely why isosmotic and isotonic are not synonyms - a distinction asked repeatedly.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-7`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A 1 per cent w/v drug solution depresses the freezing point of water by 0.20 degrees Celsius. How much sodium chloride is needed per 100 mL to make it isotonic with blood? (1 per cent w/v sodium chloride depresses the freezing point by 0.576 degrees)`,options:[`0.56 g`,`0.35 g`,`0.90 g`,`0.32 g`],answer:0,explanation:`W = (0.52 - a)/b = (0.52 - 0.20)/0.576 = 0.32/0.576 = 0.556 g, about 0.56 g per 100 mL. Answering 0.32 g reports the freezing point deficit itself rather than converting it to a weight of sodium chloride, and 0.90 g ignores the drug contribution altogether.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-8`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`By the White-Vincent method, what volume of isotonic solution is obtained from 0.30 g of a drug with a sodium chloride equivalent of 0.13?`,options:[`3.33 mL`,`33.33 mL`,`11.11 mL`,`4.33 mL`],answer:3,explanation:`V = w x E x 111.1 = 0.30 x 0.13 x 111.1 = 0.039 x 111.1 = 4.33 mL. The constant 111.1 is the volume of isotonic solution produced by 1 g of sodium chloride (1/0.009). The remaining volume of the prescription is then made up with an isotonic diluting vehicle - that step is what makes this a Class II method.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-1`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A complex in which a single ligand donates two or more pairs of electrons to a metal ion, forming a ring, is called a:`,options:[`Clathrate`,`Chelate`,`Charge transfer complex`,`Channel complex`],answer:1,explanation:`Chelation involves a multidentate ligand closing a ring with the metal ion, which makes the complex far more stable than a simple coordinate complex; calcium disodium edetate is the standard example. A clathrate is a cage-type inclusion compound with no metal-ligand coordination at all.`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-2`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Basic drugs such as propranolol and lignocaine bind chiefly to which plasma protein?`,options:[`Albumin`,`Transcortin`,`Transferrin`,`Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein`],answer:3,explanation:`Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein is the principal binder of basic drugs, and its concentration rises in inflammatory states, lowering the free fraction of such drugs. Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein but it binds mainly acidic drugs such as warfarin and phenylbutazone.`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-3`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Beta-cyclodextrin consists of how many glucose units, and why is it the least water soluble of the three natural cyclodextrins?`,options:[`Seven units; intramolecular hydrogen bonding between secondary hydroxyl groups`,`Six units; the cavity is too small to hydrate`,`Eight units; the molecule is too large to be solvated`,`Seven units; it carries no hydroxyl groups on the outer rim`],answer:0,explanation:`Beta-cyclodextrin has seven glucose units and a complete belt of intramolecular hydrogen bonds between the 2 and 3 hydroxyl groups, which locks the ring and leaves fewer hydroxyls free to hydrate, giving a solubility of only about 1.85 g per 100 mL. Alpha (six units) and gamma (eight units) lack this complete belt and are far more soluble.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-4`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The method of continuous variation (Job plot) is used chiefly to determine:`,options:[`The stability constant of the complex`,`The stoichiometric ratio of the components in the complex`,`The free fraction of drug in plasma`,`The type of phase-solubility diagram`],answer:1,explanation:`In the Job method the total moles are kept constant while the mole fraction is varied, and the maximum in the measured property identifies the combining ratio, that is the stoichiometry. Stability constants are obtained from phase-solubility, distribution or potentiometric data, which is the distractor most often chosen.`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-5`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In equilibrium dialysis for protein binding, the drug concentration measured in the buffer compartment outside the membrane represents:`,options:[`Total drug`,`Bound drug`,`Free drug`,`Protein-bound plus free drug`],answer:2,explanation:`Only free drug can cross the semipermeable membrane, so at equilibrium the outside compartment contains free drug alone; total minus free inside the bag gives the bound fraction. The main practical pitfalls are the Donnan effect and adsorption of drug on the membrane, both of which distort the free value.`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-6`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Administration of phenylbutazone to a patient stabilised on warfarin can precipitate bleeding chiefly because phenylbutazone:`,options:[`Increases the synthesis of clotting factors`,`Increases the renal clearance of warfarin`,`Increases the volume of distribution of warfarin`,`Displaces warfarin from albumin binding sites and also inhibits its metabolism`],answer:3,explanation:`Both drugs bind at albumin site I, so phenylbutazone displaces warfarin and raises the free (active) fraction, and it additionally inhibits warfarin metabolism so the extra free drug is not cleared. Displacement alone is usually transient; it is the combination with metabolic inhibition that makes this interaction dangerous.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-7`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A drug is 99 per cent bound to plasma protein. A displacer reduces the binding to 98 per cent. The free drug concentration increases by approximately:`,options:[`1 per cent`,`2 per cent`,`50 per cent`,`100 per cent`],answer:3,explanation:`Free fraction changes from 1 per cent to 2 per cent of total, so it doubles - a 100 per cent rise in free drug from a change of only one percentage point in binding. Reading the answer as 1 per cent confuses the change in the bound percentage with the change in the free concentration, which is the whole point of the question.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-8`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A phase-solubility study gives an AL type diagram with a slope of 0.20. The intrinsic solubility of the drug is 2.0 mmol/L. The stability constant of the 1:1 complex is:`,options:[`0.10 per mmol/L`,`0.125 per mmol/L`,`0.80 per mmol/L`,`1.25 per mmol/L`],answer:1,explanation:`K = slope / [S0 x (1 - slope)] = 0.20 / [2.0 x (1 - 0.20)] = 0.20 / (2.0 x 0.80) = 0.20/1.60 = 0.125 per mmol/L, that is 125 per mol/L. Forgetting the (1 - slope) term gives 0.10 and systematically underestimates the stability constant.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-9`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Silver chloride has a solubility product Ksp of 1.0 x 10^-10 at 25 degrees Celsius. Its molar solubility in pure water is:`,options:[`1.0 x 10^-5 mol/L`,`1.0 x 10^-10 mol/L`,`1.0 x 10^-20 mol/L`,`5.0 x 10^-11 mol/L`],answer:0,explanation:`For a 1:1 salt, Ksp = s x s = s squared, so s = square root of 1.0 x 10^-10 = 1.0 x 10^-5 mol/L. Quoting 1.0 x 10^-10 mol/L simply repeats the solubility product without taking the root, and 1.0 x 10^-20 mol/L squares it a second time instead.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-10`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which change would be expected to DECREASE the dissolution rate of a poorly water-soluble drug?`,options:[`Preparing a solid dispersion in polyethylene glycol 6000`,`Converting the drug to its most stable, highly crystalline polymorph`,`Forming the sodium salt of the acidic drug`,`Micronising the powder to about 5 micrometres`],answer:1,explanation:`The most stable polymorph has the strongest, lowest-energy crystal lattice and therefore the lowest apparent solubility and the slowest dissolution. Solid dispersion, salt formation and micronisation all raise dissolution rate, by producing a molecularly dispersed or amorphous drug, by raising saturation solubility, and by increasing effective surface area respectively.`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-11`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`100 mg of a drug is shaken to equilibrium with 50 mL of water and 50 mL of an oil. The oil/water partition coefficient is 4. How much drug is present in the oil phase?`,options:[`20 mg`,`40 mg`,`80 mg`,`50 mg`],answer:2,explanation:`The volumes are equal, so the ratio of amounts equals the ratio of concentrations: 4 parts in oil for every 1 part in water, that is 4/5 of 100 mg = 80 mg in oil. The 20 mg figure is what remains in the water, and 50 mg assumes an equal split, which would only happen if the partition coefficient were 1.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-12`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A weak acid of pKa 4.0 has an intrinsic solubility of 0.5 mg/mL. What is its total solubility in a buffer of pH 6.0?`,options:[`5.5 mg/mL`,`0.55 mg/mL`,`50.5 mg/mL`,`100 mg/mL`],answer:2,explanation:`S = S0 x (1 + Ka/[H+]). Ka = 1 x 10^-4 and [H+] at pH 6.0 = 1 x 10^-6, so Ka/[H+] = 100 and S = 0.5 x (1 + 100) = 0.5 x 101 = 50.5 mg/mL. The value 5.5 mg/mL is what you get at pH 5.0, one unit above the pKa instead of two, and 100 mg/mL forgets to multiply the ratio by the intrinsic solubility.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-13`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug has a solubility of 1 mg/mL in water and 100 mg/mL in ethanol. Using the log-linear cosolvency model, its predicted solubility in a 50:50 v/v water-ethanol blend is:`,options:[`50.5 mg/mL`,`10 mg/mL`,`25 mg/mL`,`5 mg/mL`],answer:1,explanation:`log S(mix) = 0.5 x log 1 + 0.5 x log 100 = 0 + (0.5 x 2) = 1.0, so S(mix) = antilog 1.0 = 10 mg/mL. Answering 50.5 mg/mL takes the arithmetic mean of the two solubilities; cosolvency is logarithmic, not linear, which is why the gain per unit of cosolvent is so steep at high cosolvent fractions.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-14`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Dissolution of a powder whose particles shrink but retain their shape as they dissolve is described by:`,options:[`The Higuchi square root law`,`The Stokes-Einstein relation`,`The Freundlich isotherm`,`The Hixson-Crowell cube root law`],answer:3,explanation:`The Hixson-Crowell cube root law, W0^(1/3) - W^(1/3) = k t, corrects for the surface area falling as the particles get smaller under sink conditions. The Higuchi square root law is the tempting distractor because it also linearises release data, but it describes diffusion of drug out of a matrix, not the dissolution of shrinking particles.`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-15`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Silver chromate dissolves to give two silver ions and one chromate ion per formula unit. If its Ksp is 4.0 x 10^-12, its molar solubility is:`,options:[`2.0 x 10^-6 mol/L`,`1.0 x 10^-6 mol/L`,`1.0 x 10^-4 mol/L`,`1.6 x 10^-4 mol/L`],answer:2,explanation:`For an A2B salt, Ksp = (2s)^2 x s = 4 s^3, so s^3 = 4.0 x 10^-12 / 4 = 1.0 x 10^-12 and s = 1.0 x 10^-4 mol/L. Taking a plain square root as if it were a 1:1 salt gives 2.0 x 10^-6 mol/L, which is the standard trap whenever the stoichiometry is not one-to-one.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-solubility-16`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Barium sulphate has a Ksp of 1.0 x 10^-10. Its solubility in 0.010 mol/L sodium sulphate is:`,options:[`1.0 x 10^-5 mol/L`,`1.0 x 10^-8 mol/L`,`1.0 x 10^-12 mol/L`,`1.0 x 10^-4 mol/L`],answer:1,explanation:`The added sulphate swamps the trace contributed by the dissolving salt, so [sulphate] is fixed at 1.0 x 10^-2 mol/L and s = Ksp/[sulphate] = 1.0 x 10^-10 / 1.0 x 10^-2 = 1.0 x 10^-8 mol/L. The figure 1.0 x 10^-5 mol/L is the solubility in pure water; the common ion effect has depressed it a thousandfold, which is exactly why barium sulphate radiocontrast suspensions are formulated with excess sulphate.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-surface-9`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Lecithin is classified as which type of surfactant?`,options:[`Anionic`,`Cationic`,`Amphoteric (zwitterionic)`,`Non-ionic`],answer:2,explanation:`Lecithin carries a negatively charged phosphate group and a positively charged quaternary ammonium group in the same molecule, so it is amphoteric and its net charge depends on pH. Sodium lauryl sulphate is the standard anionic example, benzalkonium chloride the cationic one and polysorbate 80 the non-ionic one.`},{id:`q-pp-surface-10`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A non-ionic surfactant of total molecular weight 1200 has a polyoxyethylene (hydrophilic) portion of molecular weight 720. Its HLB on the Griffin scale is:`,options:[`6.0`,`0.6`,`12.0`,`16.7`],answer:2,explanation:`HLB = 20 x (molecular weight of hydrophilic part / total molecular weight) = 20 x (720/1200) = 20 x 0.6 = 12.0, which places it in the oil-in-water emulsifier band. The value 0.6 stops at the mass fraction without applying the scale factor of 20.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-surface-11`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An oil phase requires an HLB of 10.0 and is to be emulsified with 15 g of a blend of surfactant A (HLB 18.0) and surfactant B (HLB 6.0). How much of surfactant A is needed?`,options:[`5 g`,`7.5 g`,`10 g`,`12 g`],answer:0,explanation:`Fraction of A = (10.0 - 6.0)/(18.0 - 6.0) = 4/12 = 0.333, so A = 0.333 x 15 = 5 g and B makes up the other 10 g. The 10 g answer is the amount of the low-HLB surfactant, and 7.5 g assumes an equal split, which would give a blend HLB of 12.0 rather than 10.0.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-surface-12`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An oil phase consists of 60 per cent liquid paraffin (required HLB 12) and 40 per cent cetyl alcohol (required HLB 15). The required HLB of the whole oil phase is:`,options:[`13.5`,`13.2`,`12.6`,`14.1`],answer:1,explanation:`Required HLB values are combined by the weight fraction of each oil: 0.6 x 12 + 0.4 x 15 = 7.2 + 6.0 = 13.2. Answering 13.5 is the unweighted average of 12 and 15 and ignores the fact that liquid paraffin is the larger part of the oil phase.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-surface-13`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The reading obtained with a du Nouy ring tensiometer must be multiplied by a correction factor mainly because:`,options:[`The ring is made of platinum-iridium rather than of glass`,`The contact angle of the liquid on the ring is never zero`,`The volume of liquid actually lifted, and the ring and wire radii, depart from the ideal assumed by the simple equation`,`Evaporation lowers the surface tension during the measurement`],answer:2,explanation:`The simple equation assumes the detached liquid film is a perfect cylinder; the Harkins and Jordan factor corrects for the real volume of liquid raised and for the ratio of ring radius to wire radius. The contact angle is not the difficulty here, because platinum-iridium is chosen precisely so that the liquid wets it completely and the contact angle is zero.`},{id:`q-pp-surface-14`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A liquid of density 1.0 g/cm3 rises 3.0 cm in a capillary of radius 0.05 cm with a zero contact angle. Taking g as 980 cm/s2, its surface tension is:`,options:[`36.75 dyne/cm`,`14.7 dyne/cm`,`147 dyne/cm`,`73.5 dyne/cm`],answer:3,explanation:`gamma = (1/2) x r x h x density x g = 0.5 x 0.05 x 3.0 x 1.0 x 980 = 73.5 dyne/cm. The value 147 dyne/cm omits the factor of one half, which arises from balancing the upward force acting around the circumference against the weight of the raised column.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-surface-15`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A liquid of surface tension 30 dyne/cm is placed on water of surface tension 72 dyne/cm; the interfacial tension between them is 12 dyne/cm. The work of adhesion is:`,options:[`60 erg/cm2`,`90 erg/cm2`,`114 erg/cm2`,`30 erg/cm2`],answer:1,explanation:`Work of adhesion Wa = gamma(liquid) + gamma(water) - gamma(interfacial) = 30 + 72 - 12 = 90 erg/cm2. The figure 60 erg/cm2 is the work of cohesion of the spreading liquid (2 x 30), 114 erg/cm2 adds the interfacial term instead of subtracting it, and 30 erg/cm2 is the spreading coefficient Wa - Wc = 90 - 60, which being positive means this liquid does spread.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-surface-16`,topicId:`pp-surface`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A clear polysorbate solution turns turbid when warmed above a certain temperature and clears again on cooling. That temperature is the:`,options:[`Krafft point`,`Critical micelle temperature`,`Phase inversion concentration`,`Cloud point`],answer:3,explanation:`The cloud point belongs to non-ionic polyoxyethylene surfactants: heating dehydrates the ethylene oxide chains, the surfactant loses hydrophilicity and separates as a turbid phase. The Krafft point is the mirror-image concept for ionic surfactants - the temperature BELOW which they are too insoluble to form micelles at all.`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-9`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Acacia and gelatin dispersed in water form which class of colloid?`,options:[`Lyophilic colloids`,`Lyophobic colloids`,`Association colloids`,`Coarse dispersions`],answer:0,explanation:`Acacia and gelatin are solvent-loving macromolecules that disperse spontaneously in water and are thermodynamically stable, which is the definition of a lyophilic colloid. Lyophobic sols such as gold or silver iodide need special dispersion methods and are stabilised only by charge, while association colloids are surfactant micelles that exist only above the critical micelle concentration.`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-10`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Zeta potential is the electrical potential measured at which location?`,options:[`The actual particle surface`,`The inner Helmholtz (Stern) layer boundary`,`The plane of shear between the moving particle and the bulk liquid`,`The centre of the diffuse double layer`],answer:2,explanation:`Zeta potential is the potential at the plane of shear, the surface that separates the tightly bound liquid moving with the particle from the free bulk liquid, and it is what electrophoresis actually measures. The potential at the particle surface itself is the Nernst potential, which is larger and cannot be measured directly - confusing the two is the usual error.`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-11`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In a suspension the particle diameter is halved and the vehicle viscosity is raised from 0.01 poise to 0.05 poise. By what factor does the sedimentation velocity fall?`,options:[`7-fold`,`10-fold`,`20-fold`,`25-fold`],answer:2,explanation:`Stokes law makes velocity proportional to the square of the diameter and inversely proportional to viscosity. Halving d gives a factor of (1/2) squared = 1/4, and a fivefold rise in viscosity gives a further 1/5, so the total is 1/4 x 1/5 = 1/20, a 20-fold reduction. Answering 10-fold treats the diameter term as linear (1/2 x 1/5).`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-12`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`50 mL of a flocculated suspension is allowed to stand and the floccules occupy 55 mL. The sedimentation volume F and its meaning are:`,options:[`F = 0.91; the suspension is deflocculated and will cake`,`F = 1.10; the network of floccules has swelled above the original volume, an elegant suspension with no visible supernatant`,`F = 1.10; the assay is invalid because F can never exceed 1`,`F = 0.55; about half the suspension has sedimented`],answer:1,explanation:`F = Vu/Vo = 55/50 = 1.10. Values above 1 are perfectly legitimate: the loose floccule network entraps vehicle and swells past the starting volume, so the product looks uniform with no clear supernatant. Reading F as 0.91 inverts the ratio, and 0.55 divides the sediment volume by 100 mL rather than by the 50 mL of suspension actually taken.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-13`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Homogenisation reduces the globule diameter of a fixed volume of oil in an emulsion from 10 micrometres to 2 micrometres. The total interfacial area of the oil becomes:`,options:[`2.5 times larger`,`5 times larger`,`25 times larger`,`125 times larger`],answer:1,explanation:`For a fixed volume of dispersed phase the total surface area is 6V/d, so area is inversely proportional to diameter: 10/2 = 5 times larger. Answering 25 times uses the square of the ratio, which would be right for the area of one globule but not for a constant total volume, and this fivefold increase in interface is exactly why more emulsifier is needed after homogenisation.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-14`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`By the Schulze-Hardy rule the flocculating power of a counter-ion varies roughly as the sixth power of its valency. The approximate relative flocculating powers of sodium, calcium and aluminium ions on a negatively charged sol are:`,options:[`1 : 2 : 3`,`1 : 4 : 9`,`1 : 64 : 729`,`1 : 32 : 243`],answer:2,explanation:`2 raised to the power 6 = 64 and 3 raised to the power 6 = 729, so the series is about 1 : 64 : 729 and aluminium ion is by far the most efficient flocculant of a negative sol. The 1 : 32 : 243 series uses the fifth power and 1 : 4 : 9 the square, both of which badly understate how sharply flocculating power climbs with valency.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-15`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`What vehicle viscosity is needed to slow the settling of 20 micrometre particles of density 2.0 g/cm3 in a vehicle of density 1.0 g/cm3 to 0.001 cm/s? (g = 981 cm/s2)`,options:[`0.0218 poise`,`0.218 poise`,`2.18 poise`,`21.8 poise`],answer:1,explanation:`Rearranging Stokes law, eta = d^2 x (rho(s) - rho(l)) x g / (18 v). With d = 20 micrometres = 2 x 10^-3 cm, d squared = 4 x 10^-6 cm2, so eta = (4 x 10^-6 x 1.0 x 981) / (18 x 0.001) = 3.924 x 10^-3 / 0.018 = 0.218 poise, that is about 21.8 centipoise. The value 0.0218 poise is a tenfold slip from mishandling the 0.018 denominator.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-colloids-16`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In a stored emulsion the large globules slowly grow at the expense of the small ones, because small globules have a higher solubility in the continuous phase. This is:`,options:[`Creaming`,`Coalescence`,`Flocculation`,`Ostwald ripening`],answer:3,explanation:`Ostwald ripening is driven by the Kelvin effect - the higher curvature of a small globule raises its solubility, so material diffuses through the continuous phase from small globules to large ones without the droplets ever touching. Coalescence is the tempting distractor, but it requires the interfacial film between two globules to rupture on contact, whereas ripening proceeds by molecular diffusion alone.`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-9`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A single flow time in an Ostwald capillary viscometer yields a valid, meaningful viscosity only for:`,options:[`Newtonian liquids`,`Plastic systems, once the yield value has been exceeded`,`Pseudoplastic systems, because the capillary imposes a high shear rate`,`Any liquid, provided the density is known`],answer:0,explanation:`A capillary instrument imposes a shear rate that varies across the tube and cannot be set by the operator, so a single value of viscosity is only meaningful when viscosity is independent of shear rate, that is for a Newtonian liquid. For a pseudoplastic system the number obtained applies to one unknown shear rate and cannot be compared with anything, which is why knowing the density does not rescue the measurement.`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-10`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A syrup has a viscosity of 0.05 Pa.s. Expressed in centipoise this is:`,options:[`5 centipoise`,`0.5 centipoise`,`50 centipoise`,`500 centipoise`],answer:2,explanation:`1 Pa.s = 10 poise = 1000 centipoise, so 0.05 Pa.s = 0.5 poise = 50 centipoise. Answering 5 centipoise applies only the poise conversion and then stops, and 500 centipoise multiplies by an extra factor of 10.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-11`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A liquid fills the 0.5 cm gap between two parallel plates. The upper plate, of area 20 cm2, moves at 10 cm/s under a force of 40 dyne while the lower plate is fixed. The viscosity of the liquid is:`,options:[`0.1 poise`,`0.4 poise`,`2 poise`,`10 poise`],answer:0,explanation:`Shear rate G = velocity/gap = 10/0.5 = 20 per second, and shear stress F = force/area = 40/20 = 2 dyne/cm2. Newton law gives eta = F/G = 2/20 = 0.1 poise. Answering 2 poise reports the shear stress instead of dividing it by the shear rate, and 0.4 poise divides the raw force by the velocity without using the area or the gap.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-12`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A Bingham body begins to flow only above a shear stress of 80 dyne/cm2. At a shear stress of 200 dyne/cm2 the shear rate is 60 per second. Its plastic viscosity is:`,options:[`3.33 poise`,`2.0 poise`,`1.33 poise`,`0.5 poise`],answer:1,explanation:`Plastic viscosity U = (F - f)/G, where f is the yield value read where the extrapolated straight portion of the rheogram cuts the shear stress axis: U = (200 - 80)/60 = 120/60 = 2.0 poise. Answering 3.33 poise uses the total stress 200/60 and ignores the yield value, which always overestimates U for a plastic system.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-13`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The apparent viscosity of a gel falls steadily as the shear rate is increased, and its rheogram begins at the origin with no yield value. The flow type is:`,options:[`Newtonian`,`Plastic`,`Dilatant`,`Pseudoplastic`],answer:3,explanation:`Shear thinning with a curve that starts at the origin is pseudoplastic flow, seen with polymer solutions such as tragacanth, sodium alginate and methylcellulose, where the shear aligns the long molecules. Plastic flow also thins on shearing but is excluded here because a plastic body has a yield value, and dilatant systems thicken rather than thin.`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-14`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which instrument should be used to obtain a complete rheogram of a bentonite magma, and why?`,options:[`An Ostwald capillary viscometer, because it is precise and needs no calibration`,`A cup and bob or a cone and plate viscometer, because the shear rate can be set and varied over a range`,`A falling sphere viscometer, because the sphere samples the full depth of the magma`,`A capillary viscometer under a raised pressure head, because it can overcome the yield value`],answer:1,explanation:`A bentonite magma is plastic and thixotropic, so its apparent viscosity depends both on shear rate and on the time it has been sheared; only a rotational instrument such as a cup and bob (Stormer or Couette type) or a cone and plate can drive the sample through a controlled range of shear rates and record the up-curve and down-curve. Capillary and falling sphere instruments impose one uncontrolled shear rate and are valid only for Newtonian liquids, so no pressure head makes them acceptable here.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-15`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In an Ostwald viscometer water (viscosity 0.01 poise, density 1.0 g/cm3) takes 40 s to flow between the marks. How long will a liquid of viscosity 0.06 poise and density 1.5 g/cm3 take in the same instrument?`,options:[`60 s`,`100 s`,`160 s`,`240 s`],answer:2,explanation:`From eta1/eta2 = (rho1 t1)/(rho2 t2), t1 = t2 x (eta1 x rho2)/(eta2 x rho1) = 40 x (0.06 x 1.0)/(0.01 x 1.5) = 40 x (0.06/0.015) = 40 x 4 = 160 s. Answering 240 s uses only the sixfold viscosity ratio and forgets that the denser liquid is also driven by a heavier head, which shortens its flow time.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-rheology-16`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Rigid spherical particles occupy 2 per cent of the volume of a dispersion whose continuous phase has a viscosity of 1.0 centipoise. By the Einstein equation the viscosity of the dispersion is:`,options:[`1.02 centipoise`,`1.05 centipoise`,`1.50 centipoise`,`3.50 centipoise`],answer:1,explanation:`eta = eta(0) x (1 + 2.5 phi) = 1.0 x (1 + 2.5 x 0.02) = 1.0 x 1.05 = 1.05 centipoise. Answering 1.50 centipoise inserts the percentage 2 as if phi were 0.2, and note that the equation contains no particle size term - viscosity of a dilute dispersion depends on volume fraction alone.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-9`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A granulation has a bulk density of 0.60 g/mL and a tapped density of 0.75 g/mL. Its Hausner ratio is:`,options:[`0.80`,`1.15`,`1.25`,`1.35`],answer:2,explanation:`Hausner ratio = tapped density / bulk density = 0.75/0.60 = 1.25, which sits in the fair flow band; the matching Carr index is (0.75 - 0.60)/0.75 x 100 = 20 per cent, also fair. Answering 0.80 inverts the ratio, and since the tapped density is always the larger of the two the Hausner ratio can never be below 1.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-10`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`100 g of a powder occupies 250 mL when poured into a cylinder and 200 mL after tapping to a constant volume. Its tapped density is:`,options:[`0.40 g/mL`,`0.50 g/mL`,`1.25 g/mL`,`2.00 g/mL`],answer:1,explanation:`Tapped density = mass / tapped volume = 100/200 = 0.50 g/mL. The value 0.40 g/mL is the bulk (poured) density, 100/250, which is the distractor most often selected because the poured volume is quoted first in the stem.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-11`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A poured powder bed has a porosity of 0.40 and a bulk density of 0.90 g/mL. Its true density is:`,options:[`0.54 g/mL`,`1.30 g/mL`,`1.50 g/mL`,`2.25 g/mL`],answer:2,explanation:`Porosity e = 1 - (bulk density / true density), so bulk/true = 1 - 0.40 = 0.60 and true density = 0.90/0.60 = 1.50 g/mL. Answering 2.25 g/mL divides by the porosity 0.40 instead of by the solid fraction 0.60, and 0.54 g/mL multiplies rather than divides, giving a true density below the bulk density, which is impossible.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-12`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A powder occupies 100 mL when poured and 75 mL after tapping. Its Carr index and Hausner ratio are:`,options:[`25 per cent and 1.33, passable flow`,`33 per cent and 1.33, poor flow`,`25 per cent and 0.75, fair flow`,`20 per cent and 1.25, fair flow`],answer:0,explanation:`Working from volumes, Carr index = (bulk volume - tapped volume)/bulk volume x 100 = (100 - 75)/100 x 100 = 25 per cent, and Hausner ratio = bulk volume/tapped volume = 100/75 = 1.33; both readings correspond to passable flow. The 33 per cent figure divides the 25 mL difference by the tapped volume, which is the wrong denominator for the Carr index.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-13`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Sedimentation methods such as the Andreasen pipette become unreliable below about 1 to 2 micrometres because:`,options:[`The particles dissolve appreciably in the suspending medium`,`Brownian motion competes with gravitational settling, so Stokes law no longer holds`,`The suspension becomes too viscous to sample`,`The analytical balance cannot weigh such small residues`],answer:1,explanation:`Below roughly 1 to 2 micrometres the random thermal motion of the particles is comparable with their settling velocity, so the assumption of steady gravitational fall behind Stokes law breaks down and centrifugal sedimentation must be used instead. Viscosity of the medium is a fixed property of the vehicle and does not change with particle size, so that option is irrelevant.`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-14`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Milling reduces the volume-surface mean diameter of a powder from 20 micrometres to 5 micrometres. Its specific surface area per gram:`,options:[`Doubles`,`Increases 16-fold`,`Falls to one quarter`,`Increases 4-fold`],answer:3,explanation:`Sw = 6/(rho x d(vs)), so specific surface area is inversely proportional to the diameter: 20/5 = 4, a 4-fold increase. Answering 16-fold squares the ratio, which would be right if area went as the square of a length for a fixed number of particles, but here the mass is fixed instead.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-15`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A spherical powder has a volume-number mean diameter of 10 micrometres and a true density of 2.0 g/cm3. The number of particles per gram is approximately:`,options:[`4.8 x 10^8`,`9.5 x 10^8`,`1.9 x 10^9`,`9.5 x 10^11`],answer:1,explanation:`N = 6 / (pi x rho x d^3). Converting, 10 micrometres = 1 x 10^-3 cm, so d cubed = 1 x 10^-9 cm3 and N = 6 / (3.1416 x 2.0 x 1 x 10^-9) = 6 / (6.283 x 10^-9) = 9.5 x 10^8 particles per gram. The figure 1.9 x 10^9 omits the density term, and 9.5 x 10^11 comes from writing the diameter as 1 x 10^-4 cm, a one-decade slip in the micrometre to centimetre conversion.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-micromeritics-16`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A granulation has a true density of 1.5 g/mL, a granule density of 1.2 g/mL and a bulk density of 0.60 g/mL. Its intraparticle (intragranular) porosity and its total porosity are:`,options:[`Intraparticle 20 per cent, total 60 per cent`,`Intraparticle 40 per cent, total 60 per cent`,`Intraparticle 20 per cent, total 40 per cent`,`Intraparticle 50 per cent, total 20 per cent`],answer:0,explanation:`Intraparticle porosity uses the granule density: 1 - (1.2/1.5) = 1 - 0.80 = 0.20, that is 20 per cent of pores inside the granules. Total porosity uses the bulk density: 1 - (0.60/1.5) = 1 - 0.40 = 0.60, that is 60 per cent. The difference, 40 per cent, is the interspace (void) porosity between granules, and quoting that as the intraparticle value is the usual mix-up.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-9`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A drug degrades by first order with a rate constant of 0.0231 per day. Its half-life is:`,options:[`4.5 days`,`15 days`,`30 days`,`43 days`],answer:2,explanation:`t(1/2) = 0.693/k = 0.693/0.0231 = 30 days. The 4.5 day answer is the shelf life t(90) = 0.105/k, and 43 days is simply 1/k, the mean life; all three come from the same rate constant, so the numerator constant has to be remembered exactly.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-10`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`An assay shows a solution losing exactly 5 mg/mL of drug every hour, whatever the concentration remaining. The reaction order is:`,options:[`Zero order`,`First order`,`Second order`,`Third order`],answer:0,explanation:`A constant amount lost per unit time, independent of the concentration present, is the definition of zero-order kinetics, and a plot of concentration against time is a straight line. A first-order reaction would lose a constant PERCENTAGE per hour, so the absolute loss would shrink as the concentration fell.`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-11`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug decomposes by zero order at 0.5 mg/mL per month from an initial concentration of 100 mg/mL. Its shelf life t(90) is:`,options:[`10 months`,`20 months`,`180 months`,`200 months`],answer:1,explanation:`For zero order, t(90) = 0.1 x C0 / k = (0.1 x 100)/0.5 = 10/0.5 = 20 months. Answering 200 months uses the whole of C0 rather than the 10 per cent that may be lost, and unlike first order this shelf life does depend on the starting concentration.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-12`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A second-order degradation has a rate constant of 0.02 L/mol/h and an initial concentration of 0.5 mol/L. Its half-life is:`,options:[`25 hours`,`34.7 hours`,`50 hours`,`100 hours`],answer:3,explanation:`For second order, t(1/2) = 1/(k x C0) = 1/(0.02 x 0.5) = 1/0.01 = 100 hours. The 34.7 hour answer applies the first-order formula 0.693/0.02, which is the classic error; note also that halving the starting concentration here would double the half-life.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-13`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A first-order degradation has a shelf life t(90) of 10 hours. Its half-life is approximately:`,options:[`About 6.6 hours`,`About 21 hours`,`About 66 hours`,`About 95 hours`],answer:2,explanation:`From t(90) = 0.105/k, k = 0.105/10 = 0.0105 per hour; then t(1/2) = 0.693/k = 0.693/0.0105 = 66 hours. The ratio of half-life to shelf life for any first-order reaction is fixed at 0.693/0.105 = 6.6, so answering 6.6 hours quotes the ratio itself instead of applying it to the 10 hours given.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-14`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A product has a shelf life of 24 months at 25 degrees Celsius. Taking Q10 as 2, its predicted shelf life at 45 degrees Celsius is:`,options:[`3 months`,`6 months`,`8 months`,`12 months`],answer:1,explanation:`The temperature rises by 20 degrees, so the shelf life is divided by Q10 raised to 20/10 = 2, that is by 2 squared = 4: 24/4 = 6 months. Answering 12 months applies the factor only once, and 3 months would need three ten-degree steps rather than two.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-15`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The rate constant of a degradation doubles when the temperature is raised from 300 K to 310 K. Taking R = 8.314 J/mol/K, the activation energy is approximately:`,options:[`5.36 kJ/mol`,`26.8 kJ/mol`,`53.6 kJ/mol`,`107 kJ/mol`],answer:2,explanation:`Ea = 2.303 x R x log(k2/k1) x (T1 x T2)/(T2 - T1). Here log 2 = 0.301, 2.303 x 8.314 = 19.147, and (300 x 310)/10 = 9300 K. So Ea = 19.147 x 0.301 x 9300 = 5.763 x 9300 = 53,600 J/mol, about 53.6 kJ/mol. The 107 kJ/mol answer doubles this by using ln 2 with the 2.303 factor still in place, that is by applying the logarithm base twice.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-kinetics-stability-16`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A suspension contains 50 mg/mL of drug in total, of which only 5 mg/mL (the saturation solubility) is in solution. The dissolved drug hydrolyses by first order with k = 0.010 per hour and dissolution keeps the solution saturated throughout. The shelf life t(90) of the suspension is:`,options:[`10 hours`,`10.5 hours`,`100 hours`,`500 hours`],answer:2,explanation:`Because the solution stays saturated, the loss per hour is constant: apparent zero-order rate k0 = k x Cs = 0.010 x 5 = 0.05 mg/mL per hour. Then t(90) = 0.1 x C(total)/k0 = (0.1 x 50)/0.05 = 5/0.05 = 100 hours. Answering 10.5 hours applies the first-order shelf life 0.105/k and ignores the solid reservoir, which is exactly what makes a suspension so much more stable than a solution of the same drug.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-9`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A buffer contains 0.50 mol/L of the salt and 0.050 mol/L of the weak acid, whose pKa is 4.2. Its pH is:`,options:[`3.2`,`4.2`,`5.0`,`5.2`],answer:3,explanation:`pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]) = 4.2 + log(0.50/0.050) = 4.2 + log 10 = 4.2 + 1.0 = 5.2. Answering 3.2 inverts the ratio and puts the pH one unit BELOW the pKa, which would be the answer if the acid were in tenfold excess instead.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-10`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which buffer system is the standard choice for a parenteral or ophthalmic product to be buffered at pH 7.4?`,options:[`Acetate (pKa 4.76)`,`Borate (pKa 9.2)`,`Citrate (pKa1 3.1)`,`Phosphate (pKa2 7.2)`],answer:3,explanation:`A buffer works only within about one pH unit of its pKa, and the second dissociation of phosphoric acid at pKa 7.2 brackets pH 7.4 neatly. Borate at pKa 9.2 is nearly two units away and would have very little capacity at 7.4, quite apart from being restricted to external ophthalmic use.`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-11`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Adding 0.010 mol of sodium hydroxide to 1 litre of a buffer raises its pH from 5.00 to 5.20. The buffer capacity is:`,options:[`0.002`,`0.05`,`0.20`,`0.50`],answer:1,explanation:`Buffer capacity is the Van Slyke index beta = dB / d(pH) = 0.010 / (5.20 - 5.00) = 0.010/0.20 = 0.05 gram equivalents per litre per unit of pH. Answering 0.002 multiplies the two figures instead of dividing them, and 0.20 simply repeats the pH change rather than converting it into a capacity.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-12`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`What total buffer concentration is required to give a maximum buffer capacity of 0.058, and at what pH is that maximum reached?`,options:[`0.034 mol/L, at pH = pKa + 1`,`0.058 mol/L, at pH = pKa`,`0.10 mol/L, at pH = pKa`,`0.20 mol/L, at pH = pKa - 1`],answer:2,explanation:`beta(max) = 0.576 x C, so C = 0.058/0.576 = 0.10 mol/L, and the maximum occurs only when the buffer is half neutralised, that is when salt equals acid and pH = pKa. Answering 0.034 mol/L multiplies by 0.576 instead of dividing, and quoting 0.058 mol/L confuses the capacity with the concentration that produces it.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-13`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A uni-univalent drug salt has a molecular weight of 340 and an Liso value of 3.4. Its sodium chloride equivalent is:`,options:[`0.09`,`0.17`,`0.34`,`0.68`],answer:1,explanation:`E = 17 x Liso / M = (17 x 3.4)/340 = 57.8/340 = 0.17, meaning 1 g of this drug is osmotically equal to 0.17 g of sodium chloride. Answering 0.09 uses the non-electrolyte Liso of 1.9 instead of the uni-univalent value 3.4, and 0.34 halves the molecular weight to 170; the constant 17 is itself the molecular weight of sodium chloride, 58.5, divided by its own Liso of 3.4.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-14`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`How much sodium chloride is needed to render 100 mL of a solution containing 1.0 per cent w/v of drug A (E = 0.20) and 0.5 per cent w/v of drug B (E = 0.40) isotonic?`,options:[`0.40 g`,`0.50 g`,`0.70 g`,`0.90 g`],answer:1,explanation:`100 mL of an isotonic solution needs 0.9 g of sodium chloride. Drug A contributes 1.0 x 0.20 = 0.20 g and drug B contributes 0.5 x 0.40 = 0.20 g, a total of 0.40 g of sodium chloride equivalent. Sodium chloride to add = 0.90 - 0.40 = 0.50 g. Answering 0.90 g ignores both drugs, and 0.40 g reports the equivalent already present instead of the deficit.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-15`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of a drug depresses the freezing point of water by 0.13 degrees Celsius. How much sodium chloride is needed to make 60 mL of a 1 per cent w/v solution of the same drug isotonic with blood? (1 per cent w/v sodium chloride depresses the freezing point by 0.576 degrees)`,options:[`0.27 g`,`0.41 g`,`0.45 g`,`0.54 g`],answer:0,explanation:`Freezing point depression is colligative, so a 1 per cent w/v solution depresses it by 2 x 0.13 = 0.26 degrees. W = (0.52 - a)/b = (0.52 - 0.26)/0.576 = 0.26/0.576 = 0.451 g per 100 mL, and for 60 mL that is 0.451 x 0.6 = 0.27 g. The 0.45 g answer forgets to scale from 100 mL down to 60 mL, 0.41 g uses the unscaled 0.13 degrees, and 0.54 g is simply 0.9 per cent sodium chloride for 60 mL with no allowance for the drug.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-buffers-isotonicity-16`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`By the White-Vincent method, 0.30 g of a drug of sodium chloride equivalent 0.23 is to be made up to 30 mL of an isotonic eye drop. What volume of isotonic diluting vehicle is required?`,options:[`7.7 mL`,`22.3 mL`,`26.7 mL`,`33.3 mL`],answer:1,explanation:`V = w x E x 111.1 = 0.30 x 0.23 x 111.1 = 0.069 x 111.1 = 7.7 mL of purified water, which is the volume in which the drug alone becomes isotonic. The rest of the prescription is made up with isotonic vehicle: 30 - 7.7 = 22.3 mL. Answering 7.7 mL gives the water volume rather than the vehicle volume, and 33.3 mL comes from using 0.30 x 111.1 with the E value left out.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-9`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Disodium edetate is included in eye drops and injections chiefly because it:`,options:[`Chelates trace metal ions that catalyse oxidative degradation`,`Acts as a tonicity adjusting agent`,`Raises the viscosity and prolongs contact time`,`Buffers the preparation near pH 7.4`],answer:0,explanation:`Traces of copper and iron leached from glass or raw materials catalyse autoxidation, and edetate locks them into a stable chelate so they can no longer act; it also permeabilises Gram-negative cell walls and so potentiates preservatives. It contributes negligibly to tonicity or viscosity at the 0.01 to 0.1 per cent levels used.`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-10`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Equilibrium dialysis of a plasma sample gives a total drug concentration of 10 micrograms/mL and a free drug concentration of 2 micrograms/mL. The percentage bound is:`,options:[`20 per cent`,`25 per cent`,`80 per cent`,`500 per cent`],answer:2,explanation:`Bound = total - free = 10 - 2 = 8 micrograms/mL, so percentage bound = 8/10 x 100 = 80 per cent. Answering 20 per cent quotes the free fraction, which is the number the dialysis experiment measures directly but not what the question asks for.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-11`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A protein has 2 identical binding sites with an association constant of 1 x 10^4 per mol/L. At a free drug concentration of 1 x 10^-4 mol/L, the number of moles of drug bound per mole of protein (r) is:`,options:[`0.5`,`1.0`,`2.0`,`4.0`],answer:1,explanation:`r = nu K [Df] / (1 + K [Df]). Here K[Df] = 1 x 10^4 x 1 x 10^-4 = 1, so r = 2 x 1/(1 + 1) = 2 x 0.5 = 1.0. When the free concentration equals 1/K exactly half the sites are occupied, so answering 2.0 assumes full saturation, which is only approached when K[Df] is far greater than 1.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-12`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug binds albumin with an association constant of 5 x 10^4 per mol/L, and the concentration of free binding sites is 6 x 10^-4 mol/L. The percentage of drug bound is approximately:`,options:[`3 per cent`,`50 per cent`,`75 per cent`,`97 per cent`],answer:3,explanation:`Fraction bound = K[P]/(1 + K[P]) where K[P] = 5 x 10^4 x 6 x 10^-4 = 30, so the fraction bound is 30/31 = 0.968, about 97 per cent. The 3 per cent option is the free fraction 1/31; note that even a hundredfold fall in binding sites would still leave this drug substantially bound.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-13`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In a phase-solubility study the total drug solubility rises with ligand concentration, then plateaus, and finally falls as a solid complex separates. This diagram is classified as:`,options:[`AL type`,`AP type`,`AN type`,`B type`],answer:3,explanation:`B-type diagrams indicate a complex of limited solubility: the plateau is the point at which the complex saturates the medium and any further ligand precipitates it. AL, AP and AN are all A-type curves in which the complex stays in solution throughout, AL being the straight line used to calculate the 1:1 stability constant.`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-14`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A Klotz plot of 1/r against 1/[Df] gives a y-intercept of 0.5 and a slope of 5 x 10^-5 mol/L. The number of binding sites and the association constant are:`,options:[`nu = 2 and K = 1 x 10^4 per mol/L`,`nu = 0.5 and K = 1 x 10^4 per mol/L`,`nu = 2 and K = 2 x 10^4 per mol/L`,`nu = 2 and K = 5 x 10^-5 per mol/L`],answer:0,explanation:`For the Klotz reciprocal plot, y-intercept = 1/nu, so nu = 1/0.5 = 2, and slope = 1/(nu x K), so K = 1/(2 x 5 x 10^-5) = 1/(1 x 10^-4) = 1 x 10^4 per mol/L. Taking nu as 0.5 reads the intercept as nu itself instead of its reciprocal, and quoting K as 5 x 10^-5 simply repeats the slope.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-15`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A Scatchard plot of r/[Df] against r is a straight line of slope -2 x 10^4 per mol/L with an x-intercept of 3. Its y-intercept is:`,options:[`1.5 x 10^4`,`2 x 10^4`,`3 x 10^4`,`6 x 10^4`],answer:3,explanation:`The Scatchard equation r/[Df] = nu K - r K makes the slope -K, so K = 2 x 10^4 per mol/L, and the x-intercept is nu, so nu = 3. The y-intercept is nu x K = 3 x 2 x 10^4 = 6 x 10^4. Answering 2 x 10^4 quotes K alone and forgets that the intercept carries the number of sites as well; a curved Scatchard line would instead indicate more than one class of site.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pp-complexation-protein-binding-16`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A drug of intrinsic solubility 5 mmol/L shows a total solubility of 9 mmol/L in the presence of 20 mmol/L of a ligand, on the linear AL portion of the phase-solubility diagram. The stability constant of the 1:1 complex is:`,options:[`0.04 per mmol/L`,`0.05 per mmol/L`,`0.20 per mmol/L`,`0.25 per mmol/L`],answer:1,explanation:`First get the slope: (9 - 5)/20 = 4/20 = 0.20. Then K = slope / [S0 x (1 - slope)] = 0.20 / [5 x (1 - 0.20)] = 0.20/(5 x 0.80) = 0.20/4.0 = 0.05 per mmol/L, that is 50 per mol/L. Answering 0.04 per mmol/L omits the (1 - slope) term, and 0.20 per mmol/L reports the slope itself rather than the stability constant.`,tag:`Numerical`}],n=[{id:`f-pp-solubility-1`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,front:`Parts of solvent for "sparingly soluble"?`,back:`30 to 100 parts of solvent for 1 part of solute.`},{id:`f-pp-solubility-2`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,front:`Parts of solvent for "practically insoluble"?`,back:`10,000 parts and over.`},{id:`f-pp-solubility-3`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,front:`Noyes-Whitney equation?`,back:`dC/dt = (D x A) / (h x V) x (Cs - C).`},{id:`f-pp-solubility-4`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,front:`pHp for a salt of a weak acid?`,back:`pHp = pKa + log [(S - S0)/S0]; the acid precipitates below this pH.`},{id:`f-pp-solubility-5`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,front:`Dielectric constant of water, ethanol and glycerin?`,back:`Water about 80, glycerin about 46, ethanol about 25.`},{id:`f-pp-solubility-6`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,front:`Which polymorph dissolves faster?`,back:`The metastable one; amorphous is fastest of all, the stable crystal is slowest.`},{id:`f-pp-surface-1`,topicId:`pp-surface`,front:`Surface tension of water at 20 C?`,back:`About 72.8 dyne/cm (mN/m).`},{id:`f-pp-surface-2`,topicId:`pp-surface`,front:`Which tension method cannot measure a liquid-liquid interface?`,back:`The capillary rise method.`},{id:`f-pp-surface-3`,topicId:`pp-surface`,front:`Langmuir isotherm linear form?`,back:`C/y = 1/(a x b) + C/a; slope reciprocal gives monolayer capacity a.`},{id:`f-pp-surface-4`,topicId:`pp-surface`,front:`HLB ranges for W/O emulsifier, wetting agent, O/W emulsifier?`,back:`W/O 3 to 6; wetting 7 to 9; O/W 8 to 18 (solubiliser 15 to 18).`},{id:`f-pp-surface-5`,topicId:`pp-surface`,front:`Griffin HLB formula for a non-ionic surfactant?`,back:`HLB = 20 x (molecular weight of hydrophilic part / total molecular weight).`},{id:`f-pp-surface-6`,topicId:`pp-surface`,front:`Spreading coefficient equation?`,back:`S = gamma(sublayer) - [gamma(liquid) + gamma(interfacial)]; positive S means it spreads.`},{id:`f-pp-colloids-1`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,front:`Colloidal particle size range?`,back:`About 1 nanometre to 0.5 micrometre.`},{id:`f-pp-colloids-2`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,front:`Stokes law?`,back:`v = d^2 (rho(s) - rho(l)) g / 18 eta; velocity varies with the square of diameter.`},{id:`f-pp-colloids-3`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,front:`Sedimentation volume F and degree of flocculation beta?`,back:`F = Vu/Vo; beta = F / F(infinity), where F(infinity) is the deflocculated value.`},{id:`f-pp-colloids-4`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,front:`Sediment character: flocculated versus deflocculated?`,back:`Flocculated - loose, fluffy, redisperses. Deflocculated - hard cake, will not redisperse.`},{id:`f-pp-colloids-5`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,front:`Which emulsion conducts electricity?`,back:`Oil in water, because the continuous phase is aqueous.`},{id:`f-pp-colloids-6`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,front:`Creaming versus cracking?`,back:`Creaming is reversible separation by rising or settling; cracking is irreversible coalescence.`},{id:`f-pp-rheology-1`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,front:`Which flow type has a yield value?`,back:`Plastic (Bingham) flow only.`},{id:`f-pp-rheology-2`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,front:`Pseudoplastic versus dilatant?`,back:`Pseudoplastic = shear thinning (gums, gels); dilatant = shear thickening (concentrated deflocculated suspensions).`},{id:`f-pp-rheology-3`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,front:`Which viscometers work only for Newtonian liquids?`,back:`Capillary (Ostwald) and falling sphere.`},{id:`f-pp-rheology-4`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,front:`Best viscometer for thixotropy?`,back:`Cone and plate (for example Ferranti-Shirley) - uniform shear rate, small sample.`},{id:`f-pp-rheology-5`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,front:`Convert poise to Pa.s?`,back:`1 Pa.s = 10 poise; 1 centipoise = 1 mPa.s.`},{id:`f-pp-rheology-6`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,front:`Thixotropy in one line?`,back:`Isothermal, time-dependent gel-to-sol-to-gel change; shown by a hysteresis loop.`},{id:`f-pp-micromeritics-1`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,front:`Carr index and Hausner ratio formulas?`,back:`Carr = (tapped - bulk)/tapped x 100; Hausner = tapped/bulk.`},{id:`f-pp-micromeritics-2`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,front:`Carr index and Hausner ratio for excellent flow?`,back:`Carr 10 per cent or less; Hausner 1.00 to 1.11; angle of repose 25 to 30 degrees.`},{id:`f-pp-micromeritics-3`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,front:`Porosity of a powder bed?`,back:`e = 1 - (bulk density / true density).`},{id:`f-pp-micromeritics-4`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,front:`Order of the four densities?`,back:`True greater than granule greater than tapped greater than bulk.`},{id:`f-pp-micromeritics-5`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,front:`Specific surface area of spherical particles?`,back:`Sw = 6 / (rho x d(vs)) - inversely proportional to diameter.`},{id:`f-pp-micromeritics-6`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,front:`Size range of sieving and of optical microscopy?`,back:`Sieving about 40 to 9500 micrometres; microscopy about 0.2 to 100 micrometres.`},{id:`f-pp-kinetics-stability-1`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,front:`Half-life for zero, first and second order?`,back:`Zero: C0/2k. First: 0.693/k. Second: 1/(k C0).`},{id:`f-pp-kinetics-stability-2`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,front:`Shelf life t(90) for a first-order reaction?`,back:`t(90) = 0.105 / k.`},{id:`f-pp-kinetics-stability-3`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,front:`Which plot is linear for each order?`,back:`Zero: C against t. First: log C against t. Second: 1/C against t.`},{id:`f-pp-kinetics-stability-4`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,front:`Arrhenius equation in log form?`,back:`log k = log A - Ea / (2.303 R T); slope of log k against 1/T is -Ea/2.303R.`},{id:`f-pp-kinetics-stability-5`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,front:`ICH zone and long-term conditions for India?`,back:`Zone IVB; 30 C plus or minus 2 and 75 per cent RH plus or minus 5.`},{id:`f-pp-kinetics-stability-6`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,front:`Commonest degradation pathway for drugs in solution?`,back:`Hydrolysis, especially of esters, amides and the beta-lactam ring.`},{id:`f-pp-buffers-isotonicity-1`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,front:`Henderson-Hasselbalch for an acidic buffer?`,back:`pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]); useful range is pKa plus or minus 1.`},{id:`f-pp-buffers-isotonicity-2`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,front:`Maximum buffer capacity and where it occurs?`,back:`beta(max) = 0.576 x C, at pH = pKa (salt equals acid).`},{id:`f-pp-buffers-isotonicity-3`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,front:`Freezing point of blood and of 1 per cent w/v sodium chloride?`,back:`Blood -0.52 C; 1 per cent w/v sodium chloride depresses it by 0.576 C.`},{id:`f-pp-buffers-isotonicity-4`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,front:`Formula for the cryoscopic tonicity adjustment?`,back:`W = (0.52 - a) / b, grams of adjusting substance per 100 mL.`},{id:`f-pp-buffers-isotonicity-5`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,front:`Sodium chloride equivalent from Liso?`,back:`E = 17 x Liso / M; Liso is 3.4 for uni-univalent electrolytes, 1.9 for non-electrolytes.`},{id:`f-pp-buffers-isotonicity-6`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,front:`Class I versus Class II tonicity methods?`,back:`Class I adds sodium chloride or dextrose (cryoscopic, E value). Class II adds water then isotonic vehicle (White-Vincent, Sprowls).`},{id:`f-pp-complexation-protein-binding-1`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,front:`Glucose units in alpha, beta and gamma cyclodextrin?`,back:`Alpha 6, beta 7, gamma 8; beta is the least water soluble.`},{id:`f-pp-complexation-protein-binding-2`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,front:`Job method of continuous variation determines what?`,back:`The stoichiometric ratio of the components of a complex.`},{id:`f-pp-complexation-protein-binding-3`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,front:`Stability constant from a phase-solubility plot?`,back:`K = slope / [S0 x (1 - slope)] for a 1:1 complex.`},{id:`f-pp-complexation-protein-binding-4`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,front:`Which protein binds acidic drugs and which binds basic drugs?`,back:`Albumin binds acidic drugs; alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds basic drugs.`},{id:`f-pp-complexation-protein-binding-5`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,front:`Scatchard plot - what do the slope and intercepts give?`,back:`Plot r/[Df] against r: slope = -K, y-intercept = nu x K, x-intercept = nu.`},{id:`f-pp-complexation-protein-binding-6`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,front:`When is displacement from protein binding clinically important?`,back:`Drug more than 90 per cent bound, small volume of distribution and narrow therapeutic index (warfarin, phenytoin).`},{id:`f-pp-solubility-7`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,front:`Molar solubility from Ksp?`,back:`1:1 salt: s = square root of Ksp. A2B salt: Ksp = 4 s^3, so s = cube root of (Ksp/4).`},{id:`f-pp-solubility-8`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,front:`Log-linear cosolvency equation?`,back:`log S(mix) = sum of (volume fraction x log S in that pure solvent).`},{id:`f-pp-solubility-9`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,front:`Total solubility of a weak acid at a given pH?`,back:`S = S0 (1 + Ka/[H+]); for a weak base, S = S0 (1 + [H+]/Ka).`},{id:`f-pp-solubility-10`,topicId:`pp-solubility`,front:`Hixson-Crowell cube root law?`,back:`W0^(1/3) - W^(1/3) = k t; used when particles shrink as they dissolve.`},{id:`f-pp-surface-7`,topicId:`pp-surface`,front:`Cloud point versus Krafft point?`,back:`Cloud point - non-ionics turn turbid ABOVE it. Krafft point - ionics micellise only ABOVE it.`},{id:`f-pp-surface-8`,topicId:`pp-surface`,front:`Surface tension from capillary rise?`,back:`gamma = (1/2) x r x h x density x g, for a zero contact angle.`},{id:`f-pp-surface-9`,topicId:`pp-surface`,front:`Work of adhesion and work of cohesion?`,back:`Wa = gamma(L) + gamma(S) - gamma(LS); Wc = 2 x gamma(L); spreading coefficient S = Wa - Wc.`},{id:`f-pp-surface-10`,topicId:`pp-surface`,front:`Fraction of the high-HLB surfactant in a blend?`,back:`fA = (required HLB - HLB of B) / (HLB of A - HLB of B).`},{id:`f-pp-colloids-7`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,front:`Schulze-Hardy rule in numbers?`,back:`Flocculating power goes as valency to the sixth power: Na+ 1, Ca2+ about 64, Al3+ about 729.`},{id:`f-pp-colloids-8`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,front:`Where is zeta potential measured?`,back:`At the plane of shear, not at the particle surface (that is the Nernst potential).`},{id:`f-pp-colloids-9`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,front:`Ostwald ripening?`,back:`Large globules or crystals grow at the expense of small ones, because small ones are more soluble (Kelvin effect).`},{id:`f-pp-colloids-10`,topicId:`pp-colloids`,front:`What does a sedimentation volume F greater than 1 mean?`,back:`The floccule network has swelled above the original volume - an elegant suspension with no clear supernatant.`},{id:`f-pp-rheology-7`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,front:`Plastic viscosity from a rheogram?`,back:`U = (F - f)/G, where f is the yield value on the shear stress axis.`},{id:`f-pp-rheology-8`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,front:`Shear rate and shear stress definitions?`,back:`Shear rate G = velocity/gap (per second); shear stress F = force/area (dyne/cm2); eta = F/G.`},{id:`f-pp-rheology-9`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,front:`Einstein equation for a dilute dispersion?`,back:`eta = eta(0) x (1 + 2.5 phi); depends on volume fraction, not on particle size.`},{id:`f-pp-rheology-10`,topicId:`pp-rheology`,front:`Which viscometers give a controlled range of shear rates?`,back:`Rotational ones - cup and bob (Stormer, Couette) and cone and plate. Capillary and falling sphere do not.`},{id:`f-pp-micromeritics-7`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,front:`Intraparticle porosity versus total porosity?`,back:`Intraparticle = 1 - (granule density/true density); total = 1 - (bulk density/true density).`},{id:`f-pp-micromeritics-8`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,front:`Number of particles per gram?`,back:`N = 6 / (pi x rho x d(vn)^3), with the diameter in centimetres.`},{id:`f-pp-micromeritics-9`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,front:`Hausner ratio bands?`,back:`1.00-1.11 excellent, 1.12-1.18 good, 1.19-1.25 fair, 1.26-1.34 passable, 1.35-1.45 poor.`},{id:`f-pp-micromeritics-10`,topicId:`pp-micromeritics`,front:`Carr index and Hausner ratio straight from volumes?`,back:`Carr = (bulk volume - tapped volume)/bulk volume x 100; Hausner = bulk volume/tapped volume.`},{id:`f-pp-kinetics-stability-7`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,front:`Shelf life t(90) for a zero-order reaction?`,back:`t(90) = 0.1 x C0 / k; unlike first order it depends on the initial concentration.`},{id:`f-pp-kinetics-stability-8`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,front:`Shelf life of a suspension degrading by pseudo-zero order?`,back:`k0 = k(first order) x Cs, then t(90) = 0.1 x C(total) / k0.`},{id:`f-pp-kinetics-stability-9`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,front:`Q10 shelf life equation?`,back:`t90(T2) = t90(T1) / Q10^[(T2 - T1)/10]; Q10 is normally taken as 2, 3 or 4.`},{id:`f-pp-kinetics-stability-10`,topicId:`pp-kinetics-stability`,front:`Activation energy from two rate constants?`,back:`Ea = 2.303 x R x log(k2/k1) x (T1 x T2)/(T2 - T1).`},{id:`f-pp-buffers-isotonicity-7`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,front:`Van Slyke definition of buffer capacity?`,back:`beta = dB/d(pH) - gram equivalents of strong base or acid per litre needed for one pH unit.`},{id:`f-pp-buffers-isotonicity-8`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,front:`Sodium chloride equivalent method in one line?`,back:`NaCl to add = (0.9/100 x volume in mL) minus the sum of (grams of each drug x its E value).`},{id:`f-pp-buffers-isotonicity-9`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,front:`Which buffer for pH 7.4, and why?`,back:`Phosphate - its pKa2 is 7.2, so pH 7.4 lies within pKa plus or minus 1.`},{id:`f-pp-buffers-isotonicity-10`,topicId:`pp-buffers-isotonicity`,front:`White-Vincent method, the two steps?`,back:`Dissolve the drug in V = w x E x 111.1 mL of water, then make up to volume with isotonic vehicle.`},{id:`f-pp-complexation-protein-binding-7`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,front:`Why is disodium edetate added to eye drops and injections?`,back:`It chelates trace copper and iron that catalyse oxidation, and it potentiates preservatives.`},{id:`f-pp-complexation-protein-binding-8`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,front:`Fraction of drug bound from the binding constant?`,back:`Fraction bound = K[P] / (1 + K[P]), where [P] is the free binding site concentration.`},{id:`f-pp-complexation-protein-binding-9`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,front:`Klotz plot - what do the intercept and slope give?`,back:`Plot 1/r against 1/[Df]: y-intercept = 1/nu, slope = 1/(nu x K).`},{id:`f-pp-complexation-protein-binding-10`,topicId:`pp-complexation-protein-binding`,front:`A-type versus B-type phase-solubility diagram?`,back:`A type (AL, AP, AN) - complex stays soluble. B type - plateau then the solid complex precipitates.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};